/* ---------- 10.8.6 PSYCHOSIS ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.6.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Psychosis before puberty is rare enough that most psychiatrists never see it. From about fifteen onwards the rate climbs steeply, which is why early intervention services start where they do.",
  teach:[
    "Childhood-onset schizophrenia, defined as onset before age 13, is very rare, with prevalence of the order of 1 in 10,000 or less.",
    "Rates rise steeply through adolescence. Around 4 per cent of schizophrenia has onset before 15, and around 20 per cent before 20.",
    "Peak incidence is in late adolescence and early adulthood, earlier in males by around three to five years.",
    "Psychotic experiences, as distinct from psychotic disorder, are common in the general child and adolescent population, reported by around 5 to 15 per cent depending on age and method.",
    "That distinction matters greatly: hearing a voice or having an odd belief is common in adolescence and most of those young people never develop a psychotic disorder.",
    "Psychotic experiences in adolescence do carry raised risk of later psychotic disorder, of the order of a fourfold increase, but the absolute risk remains low.",
    "Psychotic experiences are also a general marker of psychopathology, predicting depression, anxiety, self-harm and suicide as much as they predict psychosis.",
    "Risk is markedly raised in adolescents with a family history, with 22q11.2 deletion syndrome, and in those with childhood adversity or cannabis use.",
    "Ultra-high risk or at-risk mental state criteria identify young people with attenuated psychotic symptoms, brief intermittent psychotic symptoms, or genetic risk with functional decline.",
    "Transition rates from ultra-high risk states to psychosis have fallen over time, from around 40 per cent in early cohorts to around 15 to 20 per cent at two years in recent samples, probably through earlier and broader identification.",
    "That falling transition rate is important, because interventions justified on the earlier figures now treat a group in whom the majority will not develop psychosis."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How common is psychosis in childhood and adolescence?","Childhood-onset schizophrenia before age 13 is very rare, of the order of 1 in 10,000 or less. Around 4 per cent of schizophrenia has onset before 15 and around 20 per cent before 20, with incidence rising steeply through late adolescence."],
    ["Distinguish psychotic experiences from psychotic disorder in young people.","Psychotic experiences such as hearing a voice or holding an odd belief are common in adolescence, reported by around 5 to 15 per cent, and most of those young people never develop a psychotic disorder. They raise the risk of later psychotic disorder around fourfold but the absolute risk remains low, and they predict depression, anxiety and self-harm as much as psychosis."],
    ["What has happened to transition rates from ultra-high risk states?","They have fallen from around 40 per cent in early cohorts to around 15 to 20 per cent at two years in recent samples, probably through earlier and broader identification. This matters because interventions justified on the earlier figures now treat a group in whom the majority will not develop psychosis."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Peak incidence is in late adolescence and early adulthood, earlier in males by three to five years.",
    "22q11.2 deletion syndrome carries a schizophrenia risk of around a quarter to a third.",
    "Ultra-high risk criteria cover attenuated symptoms, brief intermittent symptoms, and genetic risk with functional decline.",
    "Childhood adversity and cannabis use raise risk in adolescents as in adults."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","morgan"] };

TEACH["10.8.6.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The earlier psychosis starts, the more it looks like a developmental problem and the less it looks like an illness that arrived. Childhood-onset cases carry more genetic loading and more early developmental abnormality.",
  teach:[
    "Childhood-onset schizophrenia has greater genetic loading than adult-onset disease, with higher rates of family history and a higher burden of rare copy number variants.",
    "Rare copy number variants including 22q11.2 deletion are substantially over-represented in childhood-onset cases, and genetic testing including microarray is indicated in very early onset psychosis.",
    "Premorbid abnormalities are more marked: language delay, motor delay, social difficulty and learning problems are frequently present years before the psychosis.",
    "A substantial proportion of children with childhood-onset schizophrenia have premorbid autistic features or meet criteria for a neurodevelopmental disorder, and the boundary between the two is genuinely unclear.",
    "The NIMH childhood-onset schizophrenia cohort showed progressive loss of grey matter through adolescence in a parietal-to-frontal wave, exceeding the normal adolescent pruning.",
    "That imaging finding supports an exaggeration of normal adolescent synaptic pruning as a mechanism, which fits the complement component 4 genetic finding in adult schizophrenia.",
    "Adolescence is the period of maximal synaptic pruning and myelination, which is why a developmental vulnerability laid down early manifests at this point rather than earlier.",
    "Cannabis: use in adolescence is associated with a dose-dependent increase in psychosis risk, stronger with high-potency preparations and with earlier age of first use.",
    "Earlier age of first cannabis use is associated with greater risk, which is one of the more consistent findings and is clinically usable in prevention discussion with young people.",
    "Childhood adversity, particularly sexual abuse, physical abuse and bullying, shows a dose-dependent association with psychotic experiences and psychotic disorder.",
    "Bullying victimisation has genetically informed evidence supporting a causal contribution, including discordant twin studies.",
    "Urbanicity, migration and minority status operate as in adults, and the social defeat hypothesis provides a unifying account.",
    "Obstetric complications and prenatal factors contribute, and are more strongly associated with earlier onset.",
    "Organic causes must be excluded rigorously in young people, more so than in adults: autoimmune encephalitis particularly anti-NMDA receptor, Wilson's disease, metachromatic leukodystrophy, temporal lobe epilepsy, drug-induced psychosis and brain tumour.",
    "Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis presents predominantly in young people, frequently to psychiatry first, and is treatable, so it should be actively considered in any adolescent with new psychosis plus neurological features."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How does childhood-onset schizophrenia differ aetiologically from adult-onset?","Greater genetic loading with more family history and a higher burden of rare copy number variants including 22q11.2 deletion; more marked premorbid language, motor, social and learning abnormalities; and frequent premorbid autistic or neurodevelopmental features. Genetic testing including microarray is indicated in very early onset psychosis."],
    ["What did the NIMH childhood-onset schizophrenia imaging cohort show?","Progressive grey matter loss through adolescence in a parietal-to-frontal wave, exceeding normal adolescent pruning. This supports exaggerated synaptic pruning as a mechanism, consistent with the complement component 4 genetic finding in adult schizophrenia."],
    ["Why does psychosis emerge in adolescence rather than earlier?","Because adolescence is the period of maximal synaptic pruning and myelination, so a developmental vulnerability laid down early in life manifests when these processes occur."],
    ["Which organic causes must be excluded in adolescent psychosis?","Anti-NMDA receptor and other autoimmune encephalitis, Wilson's disease, metachromatic leukodystrophy, temporal lobe epilepsy, drug-induced psychosis and brain tumour. Anti-NMDA receptor encephalitis presents predominantly in young people, frequently to psychiatry first, and is treatable."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Earlier age of first cannabis use is associated with greater psychosis risk in a dose-dependent way.",
    "Bullying has genetically informed evidence supporting a causal contribution to psychosis.",
    "Obstetric complications are more strongly associated with earlier onset.",
    "The boundary between childhood-onset schizophrenia and neurodevelopmental disorder is genuinely unclear."
  ],
  refs:["semgen","morgan","lishman"] };

TEACH["10.8.6.3"] = { free:false,
  simple:"In a young person the illness usually announces itself by things falling apart rather than by clear symptoms. The grades drop, the friendships go, the person becomes odd, and the voices come later.",
  teach:[
    "Presentation in young people is dominated by functional decline and negative symptoms, with positive symptoms often appearing later and less prominently than in adults.",
    "The prodrome is longer and more insidious in younger onset, with social withdrawal, declining academic performance, odd beliefs, perceptual abnormalities, reduced self-care and loss of friendships.",
    "Functional decline in a previously functioning adolescent is the single most important warning sign, and it is frequently attributed to laziness, drugs or adolescence.",
    "Delusions in younger children are less systematised and more fantastical, reflecting cognitive developmental level, and can be difficult to distinguish from imaginative play.",
    "Hallucinations in children must be interpreted developmentally. Imaginary companions are normal in young children, and hallucinations occur in anxiety, trauma, bereavement and in the context of high fever.",
    "Hallucinations in a child are not diagnostic of schizophrenia and most children reporting them do not have a psychotic disorder.",
    "Formal thought disorder in children must be judged against expected language development, and illogical thinking and loose associations are normal in young children.",
    "Negative symptoms and cognitive impairment are more prominent in early-onset psychosis and account for much of the functional impairment.",
    "The differential is wide: autism, particularly where social communication difficulty and unusual beliefs are misread; depression with psychotic features; bipolar disorder; PTSD with dissociative phenomena; substance-induced psychosis; and organic causes.",
    "Distinguishing autism from psychosis is a common difficulty. In autism the unusual beliefs and social difficulty are lifelong and stable, whereas psychosis involves change from a previous baseline.",
    "Change from baseline is the discriminating principle across the whole differential, which is why developmental history is essential.",
    "Trauma-related phenomena including dissociation, flashbacks and voices commenting are common in maltreated adolescents and are frequently misdiagnosed as psychosis.",
    "Assessment: full developmental history, collateral from family and school, physical examination including neurological, and investigation for organic causes.",
    "Investigation in first-episode psychosis in a young person: bloods including full blood count, electrolytes, calcium, glucose, liver and thyroid function, B12 and folate, copper and caeruloplasmin, autoantibodies where indicated, urine drug screen, and MRI brain.",
    "The threshold for imaging and for genetic testing is lower than in adults, and microarray should be considered in very early onset or where there is dysmorphism or developmental delay.",
    "EEG where there is any suspicion of seizures or encephalitis, and lumbar puncture where autoimmune encephalitis is suspected."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the most important warning sign of emerging psychosis in an adolescent?","Functional decline in a previously functioning young person: falling academic performance, loss of friendships, social withdrawal and reduced self-care. It is frequently attributed to laziness, drug use or ordinary adolescence."],
    ["How should hallucinations in a child be interpreted?","Developmentally and cautiously. Imaginary companions are normal in young children, and hallucinations occur in anxiety, trauma, bereavement and febrile illness. Hallucinations in a child are not diagnostic of schizophrenia, and most children reporting them do not have a psychotic disorder."],
    ["How is autism distinguished from psychosis in a young person?","By change from baseline. In autism the social communication difficulty and unusual beliefs are lifelong and stable, whereas psychosis involves deterioration from a previously established level of functioning. Developmental history is therefore essential."],
    ["What investigations are indicated in first-episode psychosis in a young person?","Full blood count, electrolytes, calcium, glucose, liver and thyroid function, B12 and folate, copper and caeruloplasmin, autoantibodies where indicated, urine drug screen, and MRI brain. EEG where seizures or encephalitis are suspected, lumbar puncture where autoimmune encephalitis is suspected, and chromosomal microarray in very early onset or where there is dysmorphism or developmental delay."]
  ],
  note:[
    "The prodrome is longer and more insidious in younger onset.",
    "Delusions in younger children are less systematised and more fantastical.",
    "Formal thought disorder must be judged against expected language development.",
    "Trauma-related dissociation and voices are frequently misdiagnosed as psychosis in maltreated adolescents.",
    "Negative symptoms and cognitive impairment are more prominent in early-onset psychosis."
  ],
  refs:["oyebode","nice","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.6.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The same drugs are used at lower doses, and young people get the side effects far more readily than adults do, particularly the weight gain, which can be dramatic and permanent.",
  teach:[
    "NICE NG155 and CG155: for young people with first-episode psychosis, offer oral antipsychotic medication in conjunction with family intervention and individual CBT.",
    "Psychological intervention is not optional or secondary. Both family intervention and CBT for psychosis are recommended alongside medication from the outset.",
    "Choice of antipsychotic should be made jointly with the young person and parents, on the basis of adverse effect profile, since efficacy differences are small.",
    "Aripiprazole is licensed for schizophrenia in young people aged 15 and over in the UK, and risperidone has a licence for short-term treatment of aggression in conduct disorder from age 5.",
    "Doses should be lower than in adults, with slower titration, because young people are more sensitive to adverse effects.",
    "Young people are substantially more sensitive to antipsychotic adverse effects than adults, particularly weight gain, metabolic disturbance, hyperprolactinaemia and extrapyramidal effects.",
    "Weight gain is the dominant problem. Olanzapine produces the greatest weight gain and should generally be avoided as a first choice in young people; aripiprazole and lurasidone produce least.",
    "Metabolic monitoring is essential: weight and height plotted on growth charts, waist circumference, blood pressure, fasting glucose or HbA1c, and lipids at baseline, at 12 weeks, and at least annually.",
    "Prolactin elevation matters more in young people because of effects on growth, bone mineral density accrual and pubertal development, and prolactin should be measured at baseline and when symptomatic.",
    "Bone mineral density accrual peaks in adolescence, so prolonged hyperprolactinaemia during this period has consequences that it does not have in an adult.",
    "Aripiprazole lowers prolactin and can be used to reverse hyperprolactinaemia caused by another antipsychotic.",
    "Extrapyramidal effects and dystonia are commoner in young people, and acute dystonia in an adolescent starting an antipsychotic is a frequent and frightening presentation.",
    "Clozapine is effective in treatment-resistant childhood-onset schizophrenia and is under-used in this group, with the same monitoring requirements and the same effectiveness advantage as in adults.",
    "Delay to clozapine in young people is at least as great as in adults and is a recognised quality failure.",
    "Early intervention in psychosis services accept young people from age 14 in England, and NICE specifies access within two weeks of referral.",
    "Duration of untreated psychosis predicts outcome, and reducing it is the principal rationale for early intervention services.",
    "Family intervention has strong evidence and is particularly important in this age group, since the young person usually lives at home and the family carries the care.",
    "Education is a treatment target: maintaining or resuming education, with liaison to school or college, has substantial functional consequences.",
    "Address cannabis and other substance use directly, since it worsens outcome and is common in this group.",
    "For ultra-high risk states, NICE recommends CBT with or without family intervention, and specifically advises against antipsychotic medication to prevent transition."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What does NICE recommend for first-episode psychosis in young people?","Oral antipsychotic medication together with family intervention and individual CBT for psychosis, with psychological intervention offered from the outset rather than as an addition. Choice of antipsychotic should be made jointly on the basis of adverse effect profile, since efficacy differences are small."],
    ["Why are antipsychotic adverse effects a greater concern in young people?","Because young people are substantially more sensitive to weight gain, metabolic disturbance, hyperprolactinaemia and extrapyramidal effects. Weight gain is the dominant problem, and prolactin elevation matters more because bone mineral density accrual peaks in adolescence and pubertal development may be affected."],
    ["What monitoring is required on antipsychotics in a young person?","Weight and height plotted on growth charts, waist circumference, blood pressure, fasting glucose or HbA1c and lipids at baseline, at 12 weeks and at least annually, with prolactin at baseline and when symptomatic."],
    ["What does NICE advise for young people at ultra-high risk of psychosis?","Cognitive behavioural therapy with or without family intervention, and specifically advises against offering antipsychotic medication with the aim of preventing transition to psychosis."],
    ["What is the position on clozapine in young people?","It is effective in treatment-resistant childhood-onset schizophrenia with the same effectiveness advantage as in adults, and is substantially under-used in this group. Delay to clozapine is a recognised quality failure."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Aripiprazole is licensed for schizophrenia from age 15 in the UK.",
    "Olanzapine produces the greatest weight gain and is generally avoided as first choice in young people.",
    "Aripiprazole lowers prolactin and can reverse hyperprolactinaemia from another agent.",
    "Early intervention services accept young people from age 14 in England with access within two weeks.",
    "Maintaining education is a treatment target with substantial functional consequences."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudsley","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.6.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The younger psychosis starts, the worse it tends to go, because it interrupts the years when a person is supposed to be building everything else. But early treatment changes that, which is the whole argument for these services.",
  teach:[
    "Earlier age of onset predicts poorer outcome across symptomatic, cognitive and functional domains, and childhood-onset schizophrenia has the worst prognosis of any onset group.",
    "The reason is developmental: psychosis in adolescence interrupts education, peer relationships and identity formation at the point when those are being established.",
    "Cognitive impairment is more marked in early-onset psychosis and is a major determinant of functional outcome, more so than positive symptoms.",
    "Around 15 to 20 per cent of young people with first-episode psychosis achieve full and sustained recovery, with a further substantial group achieving partial recovery.",
    "Outcome is heterogeneous, and pessimistic prognostication at first presentation is not justified. Some young people recover completely and this should be said.",
    "Duration of untreated psychosis is a modifiable predictor of symptomatic and functional outcome, which is the principal rationale for early intervention services.",
    "Early intervention services improve symptomatic and functional outcome and reduce admission over the first two to three years, and the RAISE and OPUS trials support this.",
    "The benefits of early intervention attenuate after the service ends, which has led to arguments for extending the duration of early intervention rather than for abandoning it.",
    "Relapse: most young people relapse if medication is stopped, with relapse rates approaching 80 per cent within two years of discontinuation in some studies.",
    "Adherence is a particular problem in adolescence, driven by adverse effects especially weight gain, absent insight, developmental drive towards autonomy, and reluctance to accept a diagnosis.",
    "Suicide risk is raised and is highest in the early years of illness, particularly in young people with preserved insight and good premorbid functioning who recognise what has happened to them.",
    "Physical health trajectory is set early. Metabolic changes in the first year of antipsychotic treatment predict later cardiovascular risk, and the mortality gap begins accumulating in adolescence.",
    "Educational and occupational outcomes are poor, and the interruption to education is often the single most consequential effect, so maintaining education is a priority intervention.",
    "Poor prognostic factors: earlier onset, insidious onset, prominent negative symptoms, poor premorbid functioning, cognitive impairment, longer duration of untreated psychosis, substance misuse and poor adherence.",
    "Good prognostic factors: acute onset, identifiable precipitant, good premorbid functioning, short duration of untreated psychosis, prominent affective symptoms, family support and good early treatment response.",
    "Transition to adult services at 18 is a recognised point of deterioration, and continuity through this boundary is one of the more useful things a service can arrange."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Why does earlier onset of psychosis predict worse outcome?","Because it is developmentally disruptive, interrupting education, peer relationships and identity formation at the point when these are being established, and because early-onset cases carry greater genetic and neurodevelopmental loading with more marked cognitive impairment."],
    ["What is the rationale for early intervention services?","Duration of untreated psychosis is a modifiable predictor of symptomatic and functional outcome. Early intervention services improve outcome and reduce admission over the first two to three years, though benefits attenuate after the service ends, which argues for extending their duration rather than abandoning them."],
    ["What is the relapse risk on stopping medication in a young person?","High, with relapse approaching 80 per cent within two years of discontinuation in some studies. Adherence is a particular problem in adolescence, driven by adverse effects especially weight gain, absent insight, the developmental drive towards autonomy and reluctance to accept a diagnosis."],
    ["When is suicide risk highest in young people with psychosis?","In the early years of illness, particularly in those with preserved insight and good premorbid functioning who recognise the implications of what has happened to them."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Around 15 to 20 per cent achieve full sustained recovery, with a further group achieving partial recovery.",
    "Cognitive impairment determines functional outcome more than positive symptoms do.",
    "Metabolic changes in the first year of treatment predict later cardiovascular risk.",
    "Interruption to education is often the single most consequential effect.",
    "Transition to adult services at 18 is a recognised point of deterioration."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","companion"] };

/* ---------- 10.8.7 EATING DISORDERS ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.7.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Eating disorders start in adolescence more than at any other time of life, and the number of young people presenting rose sharply during and after the pandemic in every country that measured it.",
  teach:[
    "Adolescence is the peak period of onset for eating disorders. Anorexia nervosa peaks between around 15 and 19; bulimia nervosa slightly later, in the late teens and early twenties.",
    "Point prevalence of anorexia nervosa in adolescent girls is around 0.3 to 1 per cent, with a larger group having subthreshold presentations.",
    "Bulimia nervosa is less common in adolescence than in young adulthood, since onset is later.",
    "Avoidant restrictive food intake disorder, ARFID, is specifically a childhood presentation and is common in children with autism, anxiety and sensory sensitivity.",
    "ARFID was introduced in DSM-5 and ICD-11 for restrictive eating without weight or shape concern, replacing the older feeding disorder of infancy and childhood.",
    "The sex ratio is around ten to one female to male in clinical samples, though boys are substantially under-diagnosed and present later, often after greater weight loss.",
    "Presentations rose markedly in adolescents during and after the COVID-19 pandemic, reported across multiple countries, with increases in both referrals and admissions and greater severity at presentation.",
    "Younger children present with eating disorders, including under-13s, and this group has particular medical vulnerability because of lower reserves and ongoing growth requirements.",
    "Higher risk groups: those in weight-dependent sports and dance, those with type 1 diabetes, and those with a family history.",
    "Prevalence in type 1 diabetes is around twice that in the general population, and insulin omission for weight control is common and specifically dangerous."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["When do eating disorders peak in onset?","Adolescence, with anorexia nervosa peaking between around 15 and 19 and bulimia nervosa slightly later in the late teens and early twenties. Adolescence is the peak period of onset for the eating disorders as a group."],
    ["What is ARFID?","Avoidant restrictive food intake disorder, introduced in DSM-5 and ICD-11 for restrictive eating without weight or shape concern, arising from sensory sensitivity, lack of interest in food or fear of aversive consequences. It is specifically a childhood presentation and is common in children with autism, anxiety and sensory sensitivity."],
    ["What happened to adolescent eating disorder presentations during the pandemic?","Referrals and admissions rose markedly across multiple countries during and after the COVID-19 pandemic, with greater severity at presentation."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Point prevalence of anorexia nervosa in adolescent girls is around 0.3 to 1 per cent.",
    "Boys are substantially under-diagnosed and present later, often after greater weight loss.",
    "Children under 13 present with eating disorders and have particular medical vulnerability.",
    "Prevalence in type 1 diabetes is around twice the general population rate."
  ],
  refs:["nice","icd11","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.7.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The strongest aetiological finding in the last decade was genetic, and it was that anorexia has as much to do with metabolism as with psychology. That was not what anyone expected.",
  teach:[
    "Heritability of anorexia nervosa is around 50 to 60 per cent, considerably higher than was assumed when it was regarded as a sociocultural disorder.",
    "The 2019 genome-wide association study identified eight loci and found genetic correlations both with psychiatric traits, particularly OCD, anxiety and depression, and with metabolic and anthropometric traits including insulin-glucose metabolism and body mass index.",
    "This produced the reconceptualisation of anorexia nervosa as a metabo-psychiatric disorder, which is the single most important recent development in understanding the condition.",
    "Temperament and personality: perfectionism, obsessionality, cognitive rigidity, harm avoidance and high self-control precede onset and persist after recovery, supporting them as trait vulnerabilities.",
    "Autism is over-represented in anorexia nervosa, with around a fifth of women with anorexia scoring in the autistic range, and this group responds less well to standard treatment.",
    "The autism-anorexia overlap is clinically important because rigidity, sensory sensitivity and difficulty with change require adapted treatment rather than being read as illness-related resistance.",
    "Puberty is a critical period: the pubertal increase in body fat in girls, the timing of puberty, and the emergence of body dissatisfaction all cluster in the period of peak onset.",
    "Early puberty in girls is associated with increased eating disorder risk, mediated through body dissatisfaction and peer comparison.",
    "Starvation itself becomes a maintaining cause, as demonstrated by the Minnesota Starvation Experiment, in which healthy volunteers developed food preoccupation, ritualised eating, hoarding, irritability, depression and binge eating on refeeding.",
    "The Minnesota findings mean that much of the psychopathology seen in a starved adolescent is a consequence of starvation, which is why weight restoration must precede psychological work.",
    "Sociocultural factors: internalisation of the thin ideal, social media exposure, peer weight talk, and weight-related teasing. These are risk factors of modest effect operating on a vulnerable minority.",
    "Social media and image-based platforms specifically are associated with body dissatisfaction and disordered eating in adolescents, with the association strongest for appearance-focused content and comparison behaviour.",
    "Family factors: older accounts of the anorexogenic family and of Minuchin's psychosomatic family have not been supported as causal, and blaming families is harmful and impedes treatment.",
    "Family factors do matter as maintaining rather than causal influences, particularly accommodation of the eating disorder and high expressed emotion, which is why family therapy works.",
    "Life events and transitions: change of school, examinations, loss, illness and bullying frequently precede onset.",
    "Bulimia nervosa aetiology in adolescents follows the adult picture, with dietary restraint as the central maintaining mechanism and Fairburn's over-evaluation of shape and weight as the core psychopathology."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What did the 2019 genetic study change?","It identified eight loci and found genetic correlations both with psychiatric traits, particularly OCD, anxiety and depression, and with metabolic and anthropometric traits including insulin-glucose metabolism and body mass index. This produced the reconceptualisation of anorexia nervosa as a metabo-psychiatric rather than a purely psychiatric disorder."],
    ["Why does the autism-anorexia overlap matter clinically?","Because around a fifth of women with anorexia score in the autistic range, and this group responds less well to standard treatment. Rigidity, sensory sensitivity and difficulty with change require adapted treatment rather than being interpreted as illness-related resistance."],
    ["What does the Minnesota Starvation Experiment tell us about adolescent presentations?","That healthy volunteers subjected to semi-starvation developed food preoccupation, ritualised eating, food hoarding, irritability, depression, social withdrawal and binge eating on refeeding. Much of the psychopathology seen in a starved adolescent is therefore a consequence of starvation, which is why weight restoration must precede psychological work."],
    ["What is the status of family causation theories in eating disorders?","Not supported. The anorexogenic family and Minuchin's psychosomatic family have not been substantiated as causal, and blaming families is harmful and impedes treatment. Family factors matter as maintaining influences, particularly accommodation and high expressed emotion, which is why family therapy is effective."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Heritability of anorexia nervosa is around 50 to 60 per cent.",
    "Perfectionism, obsessionality and rigidity precede onset and persist after recovery.",
    "Early puberty in girls raises risk through body dissatisfaction and peer comparison.",
    "Appearance-focused social media content and comparison behaviour show the strongest associations.",
    "Fairburn's over-evaluation of shape and weight is the core psychopathology in bulimia nervosa."
  ],
  refs:["nice","semgen","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.7.3"] = { free:true,
  simple:"In a growing child, weight loss is not the only measure and often not the first. A child who stops gaining weight, or falls off their growth centile, is in trouble even if the number on the scale has not moved.",
  teach:[
    "Failure to make expected weight gain, rather than weight loss, may be the presenting feature in a growing child, and growth charts are essential.",
    "Weight and BMI must be interpreted against growth centiles and against the child's own trajectory, using BMI centile or percentage median BMI rather than absolute BMI.",
    "A child may be at high physical risk at a BMI that would be unremarkable in an adult, because reserves are smaller and requirements for growth are ongoing.",
    "Rate of weight loss matters as much as absolute weight, and rapid loss in a child is dangerous even from a higher starting point.",
    "Presentation may lack explicit weight and shape concern, particularly in younger children, and ICD-11 and DSM-5 both permit diagnosis without fat phobia.",
    "Younger children more often give somatic explanations such as feeling full, stomach ache or fear of vomiting, and may not articulate a wish to be thin.",
    "Physical signs: bradycardia, hypotension with postural drop, hypothermia, acrocyanosis, lanugo, dry skin, hair loss, and delayed capillary refill.",
    "Bradycardia in a child is a serious sign and the thresholds are age-dependent, with a heart rate below 40 to 50 depending on age indicating high risk.",
    "Growth and pubertal effects: growth retardation, delayed or arrested puberty, primary or secondary amenorrhoea, and reduced peak bone mass accrual.",
    "Bone effects are particularly serious in adolescence because peak bone mass is accrued during this period and is not fully recoverable, so duration of amenorrhoea is a key clinical variable.",
    "Biochemistry: hypokalaemia especially with purging, hyponatraemia, hypophosphataemia, hypoglycaemia which is particularly dangerous in children, and deranged liver function.",
    "Hypoglycaemia is a specific paediatric danger, since children have smaller glycogen reserves, and it can be fatal.",
    "Assessment framework: MEED, Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, published in 2022, replaced MARSIPAN and Junior MARSIPAN and covers all ages.",
    "MEED risk parameters include BMI or percentage median BMI, rate of weight loss, heart rate, blood pressure and postural drop, temperature, muscle power on the sit up squat stand test, biochemistry and ECG.",
    "The SUSS test, sit up squat stand, assesses proximal myopathy and is quick, reproducible and informative in children.",
    "ARFID presentation: restriction based on sensory characteristics of food, lack of interest in eating, or fear of aversive consequences such as choking or vomiting, without weight or shape concern.",
    "ARFID must be distinguished from anorexia nervosa by the absence of weight and shape concern, and from normal picky eating by the presence of nutritional deficiency, weight faltering, dependence on supplements or marked psychosocial impairment.",
    "Bulimia nervosa in adolescents presents as in adults, with binge eating and compensatory behaviour, and normal weight means it is frequently concealed for years.",
    "Always ask about diabetes and insulin omission, exercise, laxatives, diet pills and vomiting, since none is volunteered."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How is weight assessed differently in a child or adolescent?","Against growth centiles and the child's own trajectory, using BMI centile or percentage median BMI rather than absolute BMI, since failure to make expected weight gain may be the presenting feature in a growing child. A child may be at high physical risk at a BMI that would be unremarkable in an adult."],
    ["What is MEED and what does it assess?","Medical Emergencies in Eating Disorders, published in 2022, replacing MARSIPAN and Junior MARSIPAN and covering all ages. It assesses BMI or percentage median BMI, rate of weight loss, heart rate, blood pressure and postural drop, temperature, muscle power on the sit up squat stand test, biochemistry and ECG."],
    ["Why are bone effects particularly serious in adolescent anorexia nervosa?","Because peak bone mass is accrued during adolescence and is not fully recoverable later, so amenorrhoea during this period causes permanent deficit. Duration of amenorrhoea is therefore a key clinical variable."],
    ["How is ARFID distinguished from anorexia nervosa and from picky eating?","From anorexia nervosa by the absence of any weight or shape concern, restriction instead arising from sensory characteristics, lack of interest in eating, or fear of aversive consequences. From normal picky eating by the presence of nutritional deficiency, weight faltering, dependence on supplements, or marked psychosocial impairment."],
    ["Which biochemical abnormality is a specific paediatric danger?","Hypoglycaemia, because children have smaller glycogen reserves than adults and it can be rapidly fatal."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Bradycardia thresholds are age-dependent; a rate below 40 to 50 indicates high risk depending on age.",
    "Both ICD-11 and DSM-5 permit diagnosis without fat phobia.",
    "Younger children often give somatic explanations rather than expressing a wish to be thin.",
    "The sit up squat stand test assesses proximal myopathy and is quick and reproducible.",
    "Ask specifically about insulin omission, exercise, laxatives, diet pills and vomiting."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudphys","icd11"] };

TEACH["10.8.7.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"For a young person with anorexia the treatment is to put the parents back in charge of eating, temporarily and with support, and then hand control back as the young person recovers. It works better than anything else available.",
  teach:[
    "NICE NG69: anorexia-nervosa-focused family therapy, FT-AN, is first-line for children and young people, and has the strongest evidence of any treatment in this age group.",
    "FT-AN is delivered in three phases: first, parents take charge of refeeding with the therapist supporting them; second, control of eating is gradually returned to the young person; third, adolescent development and relapse prevention are addressed.",
    "The therapy is explicitly non-blaming. Parents are positioned as the resource rather than the cause, and the externalisation of the illness from the young person is a core technique.",
    "Externalising the illness, separating the young person from the anorexia so the family can unite against it rather than against the child, is the technique that makes the approach workable.",
    "If FT-AN is unacceptable, contraindicated or ineffective, individual CBT-ED or adolescent-focused psychotherapy for anorexia nervosa are the alternatives.",
    "For bulimia nervosa in young people, bulimia-nervosa-focused family therapy is first-line, with individual CBT-ED as the alternative.",
    "Weight restoration must precede meaningful psychological work, since starvation impairs the cognitive flexibility that therapy requires.",
    "Refeeding syndrome: on reintroduction of carbohydrate, insulin release drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium intracellularly, producing hypophosphataemia, arrhythmia, cardiac failure, rhabdomyolysis, seizures and death.",
    "Prevent it by starting cautiously, monitoring phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily during the early phase, correcting before and during feeding, and giving thiamine before feeding starts.",
    "Underfeeding syndrome is the opposite and equally dangerous error, in which excessive caution produces continued weight loss in hospital. MEED addresses the balance explicitly and cautions against overly conservative refeeding.",
    "Medication has no established role in the core symptoms of anorexia nervosa. Olanzapine produces modest weight gain without addressing core psychopathology, and evidence in adolescents is limited.",
    "Antidepressants do not aid weight restoration and are ineffective for depressive symptoms at very low weight, since those symptoms are substantially consequences of starvation.",
    "For bulimia nervosa, fluoxetine is used in adults at 60 milligrams, and evidence in adolescents is limited, so psychological treatment is emphasised further.",
    "Admission indications: physical risk on MEED criteria, rapid weight loss, failure of outpatient treatment, significant comorbidity, and suicide risk.",
    "Admission should be to a specialist eating disorder unit or a paediatric ward with appropriate support, and admission to a general adolescent psychiatric unit without eating disorder expertise is not adequate.",
    "Legal framework: young people under 16 may consent if Gillick competent; 16 and 17 year olds are presumed to have capacity. Refusal of life-saving treatment by a competent young person cannot be overridden by parental consent, and the Mental Health Act or the inherent jurisdiction should be used.",
    "Nasogastric feeding may be given under the Mental Health Act, since feeding is treatment for the mental disorder.",
    "Capacity in anorexia nervosa is complex, since the young person may understand and retain the information but be unable to weigh it because the illness distorts the value assigned to the outcome.",
    "Involve education: liaison with school, adjustment of academic pressure, and a plan for physical education, which is frequently a site of compensatory exercise."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Describe anorexia-nervosa-focused family therapy.","The NICE first-line treatment for children and young people, delivered in three phases: parents take charge of refeeding with therapist support; control of eating is gradually returned to the young person; and adolescent development and relapse prevention are addressed. It is explicitly non-blaming, positioning parents as the resource rather than the cause, and externalises the illness from the young person."],
    ["Why is externalisation a core technique?","Because separating the young person from the anorexia allows the family to unite against the illness rather than against the child, which makes it possible for parents to take charge of eating without the relationship breaking down."],
    ["Describe refeeding syndrome and its prevention.","On reintroduction of carbohydrate after starvation, insulin release drives phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells, producing hypophosphataemia, arrhythmia, cardiac failure, rhabdomyolysis, seizures and death. Prevent it by starting feeding cautiously, monitoring phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily in the early phase, correcting deficits before and during feeding, and giving thiamine before feeding begins."],
    ["Can a competent young person refuse life-saving treatment for anorexia nervosa?","Parental consent cannot override the refusal of a competent young person. The Mental Health Act or the inherent jurisdiction of the High Court should be used instead. Nasogastric feeding may be given under the Mental Health Act since feeding is treatment for the mental disorder."],
    ["What is the role of medication in adolescent anorexia nervosa?","There is no established role in the core symptoms. Olanzapine produces modest weight gain without addressing core psychopathology, with limited adolescent evidence. Antidepressants do not aid weight restoration and are ineffective for depressive symptoms at very low weight, since those symptoms are substantially consequences of starvation."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Underfeeding syndrome is as dangerous as refeeding syndrome; MEED cautions against overly conservative refeeding.",
    "Bulimia-nervosa-focused family therapy is first-line for bulimia in young people.",
    "Admission should be to specialist eating disorder provision, not a general adolescent unit.",
    "Capacity may be impaired through inability to weigh information despite understanding it.",
    "Physical education at school is frequently a site of compensatory exercise."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudphys","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.7.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Adolescent anorexia has a much better outlook than adult anorexia, and the reason is largely that it is caught earlier and the family is still there to help. Duration before treatment is the thing that matters most.",
  teach:[
    "Adolescent-onset anorexia nervosa has substantially better outcomes than adult-onset disease, with recovery rates of the order of 60 to 70 per cent at long-term follow-up.",
    "The better prognosis reflects shorter duration of illness at treatment, availability of family involvement, and greater physiological resilience.",
    "Duration of illness before treatment is the strongest predictor of outcome, which makes early detection and rapid access the most consequential service factors.",
    "Early weight gain in the first weeks of treatment is among the strongest predictors of eventual recovery, and supports assertive early intervention rather than a gradual approach.",
    "Around 20 per cent run a chronic course, and a proportion develop severe and enduring illness.",
    "Mortality is raised, though lower in adolescent-onset than adult-onset cohorts, and anorexia nervosa retains the highest standardised mortality ratio of any psychiatric disorder overall.",
    "Deaths are from medical complications, principally cardiac, and from suicide, which accounts for around a fifth.",
    "Crossover between diagnoses occurs: a substantial minority with restricting anorexia develop binge-purge symptoms, and crossover to bulimia nervosa is more common than the reverse.",
    "Physical sequelae in those who recover: reduced final adult height where growth was arrested during a growth phase, reduced peak bone mass with osteopenia, and in some, persistent menstrual irregularity.",
    "Growth retardation may not be fully recoverable if the illness occurs during a growth spurt and treatment is delayed, which is a specific paediatric consequence.",
    "Fertility is generally preserved after recovery, though pregnancy in a woman with a history of eating disorder carries higher risk and relapse is common in the perinatal period.",
    "Poor prognostic factors: longer duration before treatment, lower minimum weight, binge-purge subtype, vomiting and laxative misuse, comorbid autism, comorbid depression or substance misuse, and poor family functioning.",
    "Comorbid autism predicts poorer response to standard treatment and requires adapted approaches, which is a recent and clinically important finding.",
    "Good prognostic factors: shorter duration, early weight gain, family involvement in treatment, absence of purging, absence of comorbidity, and younger age at treatment within the adolescent range.",
    "Bulimia nervosa in adolescents has a better outcome than anorexia nervosa, with around half recovering and treatment response typically faster.",
    "ARFID outcome varies widely, with better outcomes where the driver is a specific fear following a choking or vomiting episode, and more persistent difficulty where it is sensory and associated with autism.",
    "Educational disruption is a substantial consequence, with admission and illness frequently coinciding with public examinations, and academic recovery should be planned rather than assumed."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How does adolescent-onset anorexia nervosa outcome compare with adult-onset?","Substantially better, with recovery rates of the order of 60 to 70 per cent at long-term follow-up, reflecting shorter duration of illness at treatment, availability of family involvement, and greater physiological resilience."],
    ["What is the strongest predictor of outcome?","Duration of illness before treatment, which makes early detection and rapid access the most consequential service factors. Early weight gain in the first weeks of treatment is the strongest within-treatment predictor."],
    ["Which comorbidity predicts poorer treatment response in adolescent anorexia nervosa?","Comorbid autism, which predicts poorer response to standard treatment and requires adapted approaches. This is a recent and clinically important finding."],
    ["What physical sequelae may persist after recovery?","Reduced final adult height where growth was arrested during a growth spurt and treatment was delayed; reduced peak bone mass with osteopenia; and in some, persistent menstrual irregularity. Growth retardation is a specific paediatric consequence that may not be fully recoverable."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Around 20 per cent run a chronic course, with some developing severe and enduring illness.",
    "Around a fifth of deaths in anorexia nervosa are by suicide.",
    "Crossover from restricting anorexia to binge-purge presentations is common.",
    "Bulimia nervosa in adolescents has a better outcome, with around half recovering.",
    "Illness and admission frequently coincide with public examinations, and academic recovery should be planned."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","maudphys"] };

/* ---------- 10.8.8 AUTISM SPECTRUM DISORDERS ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.8.1"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Autism diagnoses have risen enormously and the reason is mostly that the definition widened and recognition improved, not that more children became autistic.",
  teach:[
    "Current prevalence estimates in children are around 1 to 2 per cent in the UK and internationally, with some recent studies reporting higher figures.",
    "Recorded prevalence has risen dramatically since the 1970s, when estimates were around 4 per 10,000, a rise of one to two orders of magnitude.",
    "The rise is attributable principally to broadening diagnostic criteria, the introduction of the spectrum concept, diagnostic substitution from intellectual disability, increased awareness, and service and educational incentives to diagnose.",
    "Diagnostic substitution is well demonstrated: as autism diagnoses rose, diagnoses of intellectual disability and of language disorder fell correspondingly in the same populations.",
    "There is no credible evidence of a true epidemic. Studies applying consistent criteria across time find much more stable rates.",
    "The male to female ratio in clinical samples is around three or four to one, falling to around three to one or lower in careful population screening studies, indicating substantial under-diagnosis in girls.",
    "Girls are diagnosed later and require greater impairment to receive a diagnosis, partly through camouflaging and partly through diagnostic criteria and instruments developed on male samples.",
    "Camouflaging or masking, the effortful suppression of autistic behaviours and conscious imitation of social behaviour, is commoner in girls and women and delays diagnosis at substantial psychological cost.",
    "Around a third to a half of autistic people have co-occurring intellectual disability, though this proportion has fallen as diagnosis has broadened to include more able individuals.",
    "Autism is markedly over-represented in certain genetic conditions: fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, 22q11.2 deletion and Rett syndrome.",
    "Adult diagnosis rates have risen sharply, with long waiting lists, as adults who were missed in childhood present for assessment.",
    "The MMR vaccine does not cause autism. The 1998 Wakefield paper was fraudulent, was retracted in 2010, Wakefield was struck off, and multiple large studies have found no association."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Why have autism diagnosis rates risen so dramatically?","Principally through broadening diagnostic criteria, introduction of the spectrum concept, diagnostic substitution from intellectual disability and language disorder, increased awareness, and service and educational incentives to diagnose. Studies applying consistent criteria across time find much more stable rates, and there is no credible evidence of a true epidemic."],
    ["Why is autism under-diagnosed in girls?","Because of camouflaging or masking, the effortful suppression of autistic behaviours and conscious imitation of social behaviour, which is commoner in girls; and because diagnostic criteria and instruments were developed largely on male samples. Girls are diagnosed later and require greater impairment to receive a diagnosis."],
    ["What is the position on MMR and autism?","The MMR vaccine does not cause autism. The 1998 Wakefield paper was fraudulent, was retracted in 2010, and Wakefield was removed from the medical register. Multiple large epidemiological studies have found no association."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Current prevalence estimates are around 1 to 2 per cent in children.",
    "Diagnostic substitution is demonstrated: intellectual disability and language disorder diagnoses fell as autism diagnoses rose.",
    "Around a third to a half of autistic people have co-occurring intellectual disability.",
    "Autism is over-represented in fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, 22q11.2 deletion and Rett syndrome.",
    "Adult diagnosis rates have risen sharply with long waiting lists."
  ],
  refs:["rutter","nice","semgen"] };

TEACH["10.8.8.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Autism is among the most heritable conditions in psychiatry. It is not caused by parenting, it is not caused by vaccines, and the search for a single cause has failed because there are hundreds.",
  teach:[
    "Heritability is high, around 70 to 90 per cent in twin studies, with monozygotic concordance around 60 to 90 per cent and dizygotic considerably lower.",
    "Sibling recurrence risk is around 10 to 20 per cent, substantially above the population rate, and higher where the affected sibling is female.",
    "Genetic architecture: highly heterogeneous, with common variants of small effect contributing the largest share of population risk, plus rare de novo mutations and copy number variants of large effect in a minority.",
    "Hundreds of genes have been implicated, converging on synaptic function, chromatin remodelling and transcriptional regulation. There is no single autism gene.",
    "Syndromic autism accounts for a minority: fragile X, tuberous sclerosis, Rett syndrome, 22q11.2 deletion, and 16p11.2 deletion and duplication.",
    "Advanced paternal age raises risk through accumulated de novo mutations in the male germline, and advanced maternal age also contributes.",
    "Prenatal factors: valproate exposure carries a substantially raised risk and is the clearest environmental cause; maternal rubella and other infections; extreme prematurity; and obstetric complications.",
    "Sodium valproate in pregnancy raises autism risk several-fold and is the reason for the pregnancy prevention programme, alongside the malformation risk.",
    "Neurobiology: early brain overgrowth in the first years of life in a subgroup, with head circumference acceleration; altered connectivity with proposed local overconnectivity and long-range underconnectivity; and altered excitation-inhibition balance.",
    "No neurobiological finding is diagnostic, and the heterogeneity of findings mirrors the heterogeneity of the genetics.",
    "Cognitive theories: theory of mind deficit, from Baron-Cohen, Leslie and Frith, proposing impaired ability to attribute mental states, demonstrated by the Sally-Anne false belief task.",
    "Weak central coherence, from Frith, proposing a processing bias towards detail at the expense of global context, which explains both difficulties and strengths such as superior performance on the embedded figures task.",
    "Executive dysfunction accounts propose deficits in planning, flexibility and inhibition, explaining the repetitive behaviours and difficulty with change.",
    "The double empathy problem, from Milton, reframes the social difficulty as bidirectional: autistic and non-autistic people each find the other difficult to understand, rather than the deficit lying wholly in the autistic person.",
    "The double empathy account has empirical support, since autistic people communicate effectively with other autistic people, and it has changed how the social difficulty is conceptualised.",
    "The extreme male brain theory, from Baron-Cohen, proposes autism as an exaggeration of a typically male cognitive profile of systemising over empathising. It is influential and contested.",
    "Refrigerator mother theory, from Kanner and popularised by Bettelheim, attributed autism to cold parenting. It was wrong, caused enormous harm to families, and is the standing example of aetiological speculation causing damage.",
    "Environmental causal claims about vaccines, diet, gut bacteria and screen time are not supported, and treatments based on them, including chelation and bleach-based products, have caused serious harm."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the heritability and genetic architecture of autism?","Heritability is around 70 to 90 per cent, with sibling recurrence risk of 10 to 20 per cent. The architecture is highly heterogeneous, with common variants of small effect contributing most population risk, plus rare de novo mutations and copy number variants of large effect in a minority. Hundreds of genes are implicated, converging on synaptic function, chromatin remodelling and transcriptional regulation."],
    ["What is the double empathy problem?","Milton's reframing of autistic social difficulty as bidirectional: autistic and non-autistic people each find the other difficult to understand, rather than the deficit residing wholly in the autistic person. It has empirical support, since autistic people communicate effectively with other autistic people."],
    ["Describe weak central coherence.","Frith's proposal of a processing bias towards local detail at the expense of global context. It explains both difficulties, such as missing the gist of a situation, and strengths, such as superior performance on the embedded figures task and attention to detail."],
    ["Which environmental exposure most clearly raises autism risk?","Sodium valproate in pregnancy, which raises autism risk several-fold and is the reason for the pregnancy prevention programme alongside the risk of major congenital malformation."],
    ["What was refrigerator mother theory and why does it matter?","The attribution of autism to cold, emotionally distant parenting, proposed by Kanner and popularised by Bettelheim. It was wrong, caused enormous harm to families, and stands as the example of aetiological speculation causing lasting damage."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Advanced paternal age raises risk through de novo mutations in the male germline.",
    "Early brain overgrowth with head circumference acceleration occurs in a subgroup.",
    "Theory of mind deficit is demonstrated by the Sally-Anne false belief task.",
    "The extreme male brain theory is influential and contested.",
    "Chelation and bleach-based products marketed as autism treatments have caused serious harm."
  ],
  refs:["rutter","semgen","gross"] };

TEACH["10.8.8.3"] = { free:true,
  simple:"Two things define autism: difficulty with the back-and-forth of social interaction, and a need for sameness with intense narrow interests. Both must have been there from early childhood, even if nobody noticed until later.",
  teach:[
    "DSM-5 and ICD-11 both require two domains: persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, and restricted repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities.",
    "DSM-5 collapsed the previous separate diagnoses of autistic disorder, Asperger's disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into a single autism spectrum disorder.",
    "Asperger's syndrome no longer exists as a separate diagnosis in either system, though many people continue to use the term for themselves and this should be respected.",
    "Social communication domain: deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, in nonverbal communicative behaviours, and in developing, maintaining and understanding relationships.",
    "Restricted repetitive domain: stereotyped or repetitive movements or speech; insistence on sameness and inflexible adherence to routine; highly restricted fixated interests of abnormal intensity or focus; and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input.",
    "Sensory features were added as a criterion in DSM-5, having previously been regarded as associated rather than diagnostic, and they are a major source of impairment.",
    "Sensory sensitivity is frequently the most disabling feature in daily life and is the one most amenable to environmental adaptation.",
    "Symptoms must be present in early developmental period, though they may not become fully manifest until social demands exceed capacity, which is why diagnosis can be made in adolescence or adulthood.",
    "The late-manifestation clause is important: a person may be diagnosed at 30 and still have had autism since early childhood, and this is not a contradiction.",
    "Severity specifiers in DSM-5 describe the level of support required, graded across each domain separately.",
    "Specifiers for co-occurring intellectual impairment and language impairment are used, since these substantially alter presentation and needs.",
    "Presentation in girls: more socially motivated, better superficial social skills through learning and imitation, interests that are more socially acceptable in content though equally intense, and greater camouflaging.",
    "Camouflaging is effortful and exhausting, is associated with anxiety, depression and autistic burnout, and its cost is frequently invisible until the person collapses.",
    "Comorbidity is the rule: ADHD in a large proportion, anxiety disorders in around 40 per cent, depression, epilepsy in around 20 to 30 per cent particularly with intellectual disability, sleep disorder, and gastrointestinal problems.",
    "Autism and ADHD may be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited and DSM-5 permits, and co-occurrence is common.",
    "Catatonia occurs in autism, particularly in adolescence, presenting as progressive slowing, increased prompt dependence, and loss of function, and it is treatable and frequently missed.",
    "Assessment: developmental history, direct observation, information from school, and where used, structured instruments such as ADI-R for the parent interview and ADOS-2 for observation.",
    "ADOS and ADI-R support but do not establish the diagnosis, which remains clinical, and NICE recommends multidisciplinary assessment.",
    "Differential diagnosis: developmental language disorder, intellectual disability, social anxiety disorder, selective mutism, attachment disorder, schizoid personality, and severe deprivation producing quasi-autistic features."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["State the two diagnostic domains of autism.","Persistent deficits in social communication and social interaction, comprising deficits in social-emotional reciprocity, in nonverbal communicative behaviour, and in developing and maintaining relationships; and restricted repetitive patterns of behaviour, interests or activities, comprising stereotyped movements or speech, insistence on sameness, highly restricted fixated interests, and hyper- or hyporeactivity to sensory input."],
    ["What happened to Asperger's syndrome?","It was removed as a separate diagnosis in DSM-5 and ICD-11, collapsed with autistic disorder and pervasive developmental disorder not otherwise specified into a single autism spectrum disorder. Many people continue to use the term for themselves and this should be respected."],
    ["How does autism present differently in girls?","With greater social motivation, better superficial social skills acquired through learning and imitation, interests that are more socially acceptable in content though equally intense in focus, and greater camouflaging. Camouflaging is effortful and exhausting, is associated with anxiety, depression and autistic burnout, and its cost is frequently invisible."],
    ["Why can autism be diagnosed for the first time in adulthood?","Because the criteria require symptoms to be present in the early developmental period but allow that they may not become fully manifest until social demands exceed the person's capacity. A person diagnosed at 30 has had autism since early childhood."],
    ["What is catatonia in autism?","A presentation, particularly in adolescence, of progressive slowing, increased prompt dependence, mutism, posturing and loss of previously acquired function. It is treatable with benzodiazepines and ECT and is frequently missed, being attributed to deterioration in the autism itself."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Sensory features were added as a diagnostic criterion in DSM-5 and are frequently the most disabling feature.",
    "Autism and ADHD may now be diagnosed together, which DSM-IV prohibited.",
    "Epilepsy occurs in around 20 to 30 per cent, particularly with intellectual disability.",
    "ADI-R and ADOS-2 support but do not establish the diagnosis, which remains clinical.",
    "Severe deprivation can produce quasi-autistic features, as shown in the Romanian adoptees."
  ],
  refs:["icd11","dsm5","nice"] };

TEACH["10.8.8.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"There is no treatment for autism itself and there does not need to be. What helps is adapting the environment, teaching communication, supporting the family, and treating the anxiety and other conditions that come with it.",
  teach:[
    "There is no medication for the core features of autism, and this should be said plainly at diagnosis rather than allowing families to search for one.",
    "The aims of intervention are communication, functional skills, adaptation of the environment, family understanding and support, and treatment of co-occurring conditions.",
    "NICE CG170 recommends a psychosocial intervention for the core features of social communication, delivered through play-based strategies with parents, carers and teachers to increase joint attention, engagement and reciprocal communication.",
    "The Preschool Autism Communication Trial, PACT, showed that a parent-mediated intervention improved parent-child interaction and produced sustained reduction in autism symptom severity at six-year follow-up.",
    "PACT is important as the strongest UK evidence for an early intervention and as an example of working through parents rather than directly with the child.",
    "Speech and language therapy is central, including augmentative and alternative communication such as PECS and communication devices for those with limited speech.",
    "Introducing augmentative communication does not delay speech development, a common parental fear, and evidence indicates it supports it.",
    "Applied behaviour analysis based approaches are widely used, particularly in the United States, and are contested. Intensive early behavioural intervention has evidence for improving cognitive and adaptive outcomes, while the autistic community has raised serious ethical objections to compliance-focused practice.",
    "That controversy should be presented fairly: there is evidence of benefit for some outcomes and there are substantive objections from autistic adults about harm and about goals focused on appearing non-autistic.",
    "Educational provision is the principal intervention for most children: appropriate setting, adaptations, a communication-friendly environment, and an Education, Health and Care Plan where needed.",
    "Sensory adaptation is frequently the highest-yield intervention: reducing noise, lighting adjustment, quiet spaces, and predictable routines.",
    "Treat co-occurring conditions on their own merits. ADHD responds to standard treatment though with more adverse effects; anxiety responds to adapted CBT; and epilepsy requires standard management.",
    "Adapted CBT for anxiety in autistic children works, with adaptations including more concrete and visual material, greater parental involvement, incorporation of special interests, and attention to sensory triggers.",
    "Medication for irritability and aggression: risperidone and aripiprazole are licensed in some jurisdictions and have evidence for irritability associated with autism, affecting irritability rather than core features.",
    "Antipsychotics in this group carry substantial metabolic burden and require monitoring, and should be used at the lowest dose with a review date, in line with STOMP and STAMP.",
    "Melatonin has good evidence for sleep-onset difficulty in autistic children and is widely and appropriately used.",
    "Interventions without evidence that families encounter: exclusion diets, secretin, chelation, hyperbaric oxygen, and bleach-based products marketed as treatments. Several are harmful and chelation has caused deaths.",
    "Discussing these directly and non-judgementally at diagnosis is protective, since families are targeted by those selling them.",
    "Support the family: explanation, practical advice, carer support, respite, benefits advice, and connection to parent networks and to autistic-led organisations.",
    "The neurodiversity perspective, that autism is a difference requiring accommodation rather than a disorder requiring cure, is held by many autistic people and should inform practice, while not obscuring the genuine support needs of those with high dependency."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What did the PACT trial show?","That a parent-mediated communication intervention for pre-school autistic children improved parent-child interaction and produced sustained reduction in autism symptom severity at six-year follow-up. It is the strongest UK evidence for an early autism intervention and works through parents rather than directly with the child."],
    ["What is the position on applied behaviour analysis?","Contested. Intensive early behavioural intervention has evidence for improving cognitive and adaptive outcomes, while autistic adults have raised substantive ethical objections to compliance-focused practice and to goals directed at appearing non-autistic. Both should be presented fairly."],
    ["How is CBT adapted for anxiety in autistic children?","With more concrete and visual material, greater parental involvement, incorporation of the child's special interests, longer duration, and explicit attention to sensory triggers. Adapted CBT for anxiety in autism has good evidence."],
    ["Which interventions marketed to families are harmful?","Chelation, which has caused deaths; bleach-based products sold as miracle mineral solution; hyperbaric oxygen; secretin, which was tested and found ineffective; and restrictive exclusion diets. Discussing these directly and non-judgementally at diagnosis is protective, since families are actively targeted."],
    ["Does augmentative communication delay speech?","No. Introducing systems such as PECS or communication devices does not delay speech development, a common parental fear, and the evidence indicates it supports rather than hinders it."]
  ],
  note:[
    "There is no medication for the core features of autism.",
    "Sensory adaptation is frequently the highest-yield intervention.",
    "Risperidone and aripiprazole address irritability rather than core features, with substantial metabolic burden.",
    "Melatonin has good evidence for sleep-onset difficulty in autistic children.",
    "The neurodiversity perspective should inform practice without obscuring high support needs."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","maudsley"] };

TEACH["10.8.8.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Autism does not go away, and it does not need to. What changes is how well the person's life fits them, and that depends far more on what is around them than on the autism itself.",
  teach:[
    "Autism is lifelong. Core features persist, though presentation changes with development, learning and compensation.",
    "A minority, of the order of 5 to 20 per cent in some cohorts, no longer meet diagnostic criteria in later childhood or adulthood, described as optimal outcome, though most retain some difficulties.",
    "The optimal outcome finding is genuine but contested, and it should not be presented as recovery or used to imply that persistence indicates inadequate intervention.",
    "Outcome is best predicted by early language development and by cognitive ability. Useful speech by age five and IQ above 70 predict substantially better adult outcomes.",
    "Adult outcomes remain poor on conventional measures: employment rates in autistic adults are very low, with figures around 20 to 30 per cent in UK surveys, the lowest of any disability group.",
    "The employment figure is the starkest outcome statistic in this area and reflects workplace environments and recruitment practice as much as individual capacity.",
    "Independent living is achieved by a minority, and many autistic adults live with family or in supported settings.",
    "Mental health outcomes are poor: rates of anxiety, depression and self-harm are markedly raised, and this is the principal cause of morbidity in autistic adults.",
    "Suicide risk is substantially raised in autistic people without intellectual disability, with several studies finding rates many times the general population, and autism is over-represented among those dying by suicide.",
    "That suicide finding has changed practice and should be known: autistic adults, particularly women and those diagnosed late, are a high-risk group and are frequently poorly served by mental health services.",
    "Mortality overall is raised, with reduced life expectancy driven by epilepsy in those with intellectual disability and by suicide in those without.",
    "Autistic burnout, a state of exhaustion, loss of skills and reduced tolerance to stimulus following prolonged camouflaging and stress, is described by autistic adults and is increasingly recognised clinically.",
    "Camouflaging predicts poorer mental health outcomes, and the psychological cost of sustained masking is a major contributor to adult morbidity.",
    "Positive outcomes are under-reported. Many autistic adults describe good lives, valued relationships, meaningful work matching their strengths, and identity as a positive rather than a deficit.",
    "Environment predicts outcome more than autism severity does. Accommodation, understanding, sensory adaptation and matched occupation change outcomes substantially.",
    "That principle is the practical conclusion of this whole section: the modifiable variable is usually the environment, not the person.",
    "Diagnosis itself has value into adulthood: self-understanding, access to adjustments, connection to community, and reinterpretation of a lifetime of difficulty, and late diagnosis is frequently described as transformative."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What predicts adult outcome in autism?","Early language development and cognitive ability, with useful speech by age five and IQ above 70 predicting substantially better adult outcomes. However, environment, accommodation and matched occupation predict outcome more than autism severity does."],
    ["What is the employment outcome in autistic adults?","Employment rates of around 20 to 30 per cent in UK surveys, the lowest of any disability group. This reflects workplace environments and recruitment practice as much as individual capacity."],
    ["What is the suicide risk in autism?","Substantially raised, particularly in autistic people without intellectual disability, with several studies finding rates many times the general population. Autistic adults, especially women and those diagnosed late, are a high-risk group and are frequently poorly served by mental health services."],
    ["What is autistic burnout?","A state of exhaustion, loss of previously held skills and reduced tolerance to sensory and social stimulus, following prolonged camouflaging and accumulated stress. It is described by autistic adults and is increasingly recognised clinically."],
    ["What is optimal outcome and how should it be described?","The finding that a minority, of the order of 5 to 20 per cent in some cohorts, no longer meet diagnostic criteria in later childhood or adulthood, though most retain some difficulties. It is genuine but contested and should not be presented as recovery or used to imply that persistence reflects inadequate intervention."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Autism is lifelong; presentation changes with development, learning and compensation.",
    "Camouflaging predicts poorer mental health outcomes.",
    "Mortality is raised, driven by epilepsy where there is intellectual disability and by suicide where there is not.",
    "Many autistic adults describe good lives and identity as a positive rather than a deficit.",
    "Late diagnosis is frequently described as transformative through self-understanding and access to adjustments."
  ],
  refs:["rutter","nice","companion"] };

/* ---------- 10.8.9 SUBSTANCE MISUSE ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.9.1"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Most adolescents who try drugs stop on their own. The ones who do not are usually the ones who started youngest and who had other problems first.",
  teach:[
    "Experimentation in adolescence is common and most of it does not progress to a disorder. Around a third to a half of 15-year-olds in UK surveys report having tried alcohol.",
    "Alcohol use in UK adolescents has declined substantially since the early 2000s, a sustained and under-reported trend seen across many high-income countries.",
    "Cannabis is the most used illicit drug in adolescents, with lifetime use around 15 to 20 per cent in UK 15-year-olds.",
    "Nitrous oxide use rose sharply among young people and was made a Class C controlled drug in the UK in 2023 following concern about neurological harm.",
    "Vaping has risen sharply among adolescents, including in those who never smoked, and disposable single-use vapes drove much of the increase before regulatory action.",
    "Age of first use is the strongest single predictor of later substance use disorder. Use before age 15 substantially raises the risk of dependence, and each year of delay reduces it.",
    "That relationship is the basis for prevention aimed at delay rather than at abstinence, which is a more achievable and better-evidenced target.",
    "Substance use disorder in adolescents is much less common than experimentation, affecting a small percentage, with higher rates in specific populations.",
    "Rates are markedly raised in looked-after children, those excluded from school, those in youth justice, and those with mental disorder, particularly conduct disorder and ADHD.",
    "Comorbidity is the rule in adolescents presenting to services: conduct disorder, ADHD, depression, anxiety, PTSD and emerging personality difficulty.",
    "Adolescent substance use is more often polydrug use than in adults, with less established patterns and greater variability."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the strongest predictor of later substance use disorder?","Age of first use. Use before age 15 substantially raises the risk of dependence, and each year of delay reduces it. This is the basis for prevention aimed at delaying initiation rather than at abstinence."],
    ["What are the recent trends in adolescent substance use in the UK?","Alcohol use has declined substantially since the early 2000s, a sustained trend seen across many high-income countries. Cannabis remains the most used illicit drug. Nitrous oxide use rose and it was made a Class C controlled drug in 2023. Vaping has risen sharply including among those who never smoked."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Most adolescent experimentation does not progress to a disorder.",
    "Around a third to a half of UK 15-year-olds report having tried alcohol.",
    "Rates are markedly raised in looked-after children, excluded pupils and the youth justice population.",
    "Adolescent use is more often polydrug and less patterned than adult use."
  ],
  refs:["semaddict","nice","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.9.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"The adolescent brain is built to seek reward and not yet built to stop itself, and drugs arrive exactly in that gap. That is why starting young matters so much more than starting at thirty.",
  teach:[
    "The developmental mismatch model: limbic reward systems mature earlier than prefrontal control systems, producing a period of heightened reward sensitivity with immature regulation.",
    "This mismatch explains adolescent risk-taking generally and makes adolescence a period of specific vulnerability to substances.",
    "The adolescent brain is more sensitive to the rewarding effects of substances and less sensitive to their aversive effects, including sedation and hangover, which permits higher consumption.",
    "Adolescent exposure produces greater and more persistent neuroadaptation than adult exposure, which is the neurobiological basis for age of first use predicting dependence.",
    "Peer influence is the dominant proximal factor. Peer substance use is the strongest single correlate of adolescent use, operating through both selection and socialisation.",
    "Selection means adolescents choose peers who share their behaviour; socialisation means peers influence behaviour. Both operate and separating them requires longitudinal design.",
    "Family factors: parental substance use, parental attitudes, poor monitoring and supervision, family conflict, and low parental warmth.",
    "Parental monitoring is the most consistently protective family factor and is modifiable, which makes it a target for intervention.",
    "Individual factors: sensation seeking, impulsivity, conduct problems, ADHD, early puberty, and academic disengagement.",
    "Conduct disorder is the strongest psychiatric predictor of adolescent substance use disorder, more so than depression or anxiety.",
    "ADHD raises risk, though the evidence indicates that treating ADHD with stimulants does not increase and may reduce later substance misuse.",
    "Self-medication: depression, anxiety, PTSD and undiagnosed ADHD all contribute, particularly to continuation rather than initiation.",
    "Trauma and adversity: childhood maltreatment shows a dose-dependent relationship with adolescent substance use, mediated through emotional dysregulation and through the environments in which maltreated young people find themselves.",
    "Availability, price and social norms operate at population level, and are the levers of effective prevention.",
    "Gateway hypothesis: the observation that cannabis use precedes other illicit drug use. The sequence is real but the causal interpretation is contested, since common liability and shared risk factors account for much of the association.",
    "The honest position on the gateway hypothesis is that the sequence is well documented and the causal claim is not established.",
    "Cannabis and psychosis in adolescents: dose-dependent association, stronger with high-potency preparations and with earlier first use, with causal contribution probable but confounding not fully excluded."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Explain the developmental mismatch model.","Limbic and reward systems mature earlier in adolescence than prefrontal control systems, producing a period of heightened reward sensitivity with immature regulatory capacity. The adolescent brain is also more sensitive to the rewarding effects of substances and less sensitive to their aversive effects, permitting higher consumption."],
    ["What is the strongest proximal correlate of adolescent substance use?","Peer substance use, operating through both selection, in which adolescents choose peers who share their behaviour, and socialisation, in which peers influence behaviour. Both processes operate and separating them requires longitudinal design."],
    ["Which psychiatric disorder most strongly predicts adolescent substance use disorder?","Conduct disorder, more strongly than depression or anxiety. ADHD also raises risk, though treating ADHD with stimulants does not increase and may reduce later substance misuse."],
    ["What is the status of the gateway hypothesis?","The observed sequence in which cannabis use precedes other illicit drug use is well documented, but the causal interpretation is contested, since common liability and shared risk factors account for much of the association. The sequence is real; the causal claim is not established."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Adolescent exposure produces greater and more persistent neuroadaptation than adult exposure.",
    "Parental monitoring is the most consistently protective and modifiable family factor.",
    "Childhood maltreatment shows a dose-dependent relationship with adolescent substance use.",
    "Availability, price and social norms are the population-level levers of prevention.",
    "Cannabis and psychosis show a dose-dependent association, stronger with earlier first use."
  ],
  refs:["semaddict","rutter","morgan"] };

TEACH["10.8.9.3"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Adolescents rarely present saying they have a drug problem. They present through school, the police, an overdose, or a parent who found something, and the substance use is discovered rather than reported.",
  teach:[
    "Presentation is rarely self-referred. Adolescents present through school concerns, criminal justice contact, parental discovery, acute intoxication, or an unrelated health problem.",
    "Ask every adolescent about substance use as part of routine assessment, in a non-judgemental way, alone and with confidentiality explained.",
    "Confidentiality must be explained accurately at the outset: what will be kept confidential, and what will be shared and with whom, particularly where safeguarding thresholds are met.",
    "The HEEADSSS framework structures adolescent psychosocial assessment: Home, Education and employment, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide and depression, and Safety.",
    "HEEADSSS moves from least to most sensitive topics, which improves disclosure, and is the standard adolescent assessment structure.",
    "Presenting features: declining school performance, school exclusion, change in peer group, mood change, sleep change, weight change, unexplained money or possessions, and physical signs.",
    "Dependence in adolescents is less often physiologically established than in adults, and the diagnostic emphasis is on harm, loss of control and priority given to use rather than on tolerance and withdrawal.",
    "Applying adult dependence criteria to adolescents under-identifies problems, because tolerance and withdrawal take time to develop.",
    "Assess the pattern in detail: substance, quantity, frequency, route, context, cost, source, and whether use is solitary or social.",
    "Solitary use and use to manage mood are markers of greater severity in adolescents, more so than quantity.",
    "Assess for exploitation. County lines drug distribution recruits vulnerable young people who are then criminally and sexually exploited, and the young person usually does not identify as a victim.",
    "Any adolescent found carrying drugs or money, travelling unexplained distances, or with unexplained possessions requires a safeguarding referral for possible criminal exploitation.",
    "Assess mental health comorbidity systematically: conduct disorder, ADHD, depression, anxiety, PTSD, self-harm and psychosis.",
    "Assess physical health: injection sites where relevant, dental health, weight, and blood-borne virus risk.",
    "Investigation: urine drug screen where clinically indicated and with consent, and consideration of blood-borne virus screening where there has been injecting.",
    "Assess the family and the wider context: parental substance use, supervision, housing, education status, and involvement with social care and youth justice.",
    "Education status is critical. School exclusion is both a consequence and a driver of escalating substance use, and maintaining education is protective."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the HEEADSSS framework?","A structure for adolescent psychosocial assessment covering Home, Education and employment, Eating, Activities, Drugs, Sexuality, Suicide and depression, and Safety. It moves from least to most sensitive topics, which improves disclosure."],
    ["Why does applying adult dependence criteria to adolescents under-identify problems?","Because tolerance and withdrawal take time to develop and are less often established in adolescents. The diagnostic emphasis should instead be on harm, loss of control and the priority given to substance use."],
    ["Which features indicate greater severity in adolescent substance use?","Solitary use rather than social use, and use to manage mood rather than for pleasure. These are more informative markers of severity than quantity consumed."],
    ["What should prompt a safeguarding referral for criminal exploitation?","An adolescent found carrying drugs or unexplained money, travelling unexplained distances, or with unexplained possessions. County lines distribution recruits vulnerable young people who are then criminally and sexually exploited, and the young person usually does not identify as a victim."],
    ["How should confidentiality be handled with an adolescent?","Explained accurately at the outset, before disclosure: what will be kept confidential, and what will be shared, with whom and in what circumstances, particularly where safeguarding thresholds are met."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Adolescents rarely self-refer and are usually discovered rather than presenting.",
    "School exclusion is both a consequence and a driver of escalating substance use.",
    "Assess substance, quantity, frequency, route, context, cost, source and whether use is solitary.",
    "Consider blood-borne virus screening where there has been injecting."
  ],
  refs:["semaddict","nice","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.9.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Treatment for young people is mostly family work and practical help with school and housing, not detox and not medication. The evidence supports working with the system the young person lives in.",
  teach:[
    "Family-based interventions have the strongest evidence in adolescent substance misuse: multidimensional family therapy, functional family therapy, brief strategic family therapy and multisystemic therapy.",
    "The common element is working with the family and the wider system rather than treating the young person in isolation, which distinguishes adolescent from adult addiction treatment.",
    "Motivational interviewing and motivational enhancement therapy are effective in adolescents and are well suited to a group with low problem recognition.",
    "CBT-based relapse prevention has evidence, and combining motivational and CBT approaches is common practice.",
    "Contingency management has evidence in adolescents as in adults, providing tangible incentives for verified abstinence.",
    "Brief interventions in non-specialist settings, including emergency departments and schools, have evidence for reducing consumption in adolescents.",
    "Avoid group interventions that bring substance-using adolescents together without strong structure, because of deviant peer contagion, which has produced worse outcomes in several trials.",
    "Pharmacological treatment is used far less than in adults, with limited evidence and mostly off-label use.",
    "Opioid substitution treatment in under-18s is used cautiously, is uncommon in the UK given low rates of adolescent opioid dependence, and requires specialist involvement.",
    "Detoxification is rarely required in adolescents, since physiological dependence is less often established, and it should not be the framework in which treatment is conceived.",
    "Nicotine replacement therapy is licensed from age 12 in the UK and should be offered to adolescents who smoke.",
    "Treat comorbidity: ADHD, depression, anxiety and PTSD are common, treatable, and frequently drive continued use. Treating ADHD in particular improves substance outcomes.",
    "Withholding ADHD treatment because of substance misuse is a common error. Untreated ADHD worsens substance outcomes, and stimulant treatment does not increase misuse.",
    "Address the practical: education, housing, family relationships, criminal justice involvement, and exploitation. These frequently determine outcome more than any therapy.",
    "Maintaining or restoring education is one of the highest-value interventions available.",
    "Youth-specific services matter. Adult drug services are inappropriate for adolescents, both developmentally and because of exposure to older dependent users.",
    "Harm reduction applies: safer using advice, naloxone where opioids are involved, blood-borne virus screening and vaccination, and sexual health advice.",
    "Confidentiality and consent: assess Gillick competence, involve parents wherever possible with the young person's agreement, and share information where safeguarding requires it.",
    "Prevention: universal school programmes have small effects; delaying initiation is a more achievable target than abstinence; and structural measures on price and availability have the strongest evidence."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Which interventions have the strongest evidence in adolescent substance misuse?","Family-based interventions: multidimensional family therapy, functional family therapy, brief strategic family therapy and multisystemic therapy. Their common element is working with the family and wider system rather than treating the young person in isolation, which distinguishes adolescent from adult addiction treatment."],
    ["Why should ADHD be treated in an adolescent with substance misuse?","Because untreated ADHD worsens substance outcomes, and stimulant treatment does not increase and may reduce substance misuse. Withholding ADHD treatment because of substance misuse is a common and harmful error."],
    ["Which group intervention approach should be avoided and why?","Unstructured group treatment bringing substance-using adolescents together, because of deviant peer contagion, which has produced worse outcomes than no treatment in several trials."],
    ["How does pharmacological treatment differ in adolescents?","It is used far less, with limited evidence and mostly off-label. Detoxification is rarely required since physiological dependence is less often established. Opioid substitution in under-18s is uncommon in the UK and requires specialist involvement. Nicotine replacement therapy is licensed from age 12."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Motivational interviewing suits a group with low problem recognition.",
    "Contingency management has evidence in adolescents as in adults.",
    "Maintaining or restoring education is among the highest-value interventions.",
    "Adult drug services are developmentally inappropriate and expose young people to older dependent users.",
    "Prevention aimed at delaying initiation is more achievable than abstinence."
  ],
  refs:["nice","semaddict","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.9.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Most young people who use substances stop as they take on adult roles. The ones who do not are usually those who started earliest, had a conduct disorder first, and lost their education along the way.",
  teach:[
    "Most adolescent substance use does not persist. Natural maturing out occurs as young people take on employment, partnership and parenting roles.",
    "The transition to adult roles is the mechanism of desistance, which is the same finding as in the offending literature and reflects the same process.",
    "Persistence is predicted by earlier age of first use, greater frequency and quantity, polydrug use, comorbid conduct disorder or ADHD, family substance use, and loss of education.",
    "Early-onset use, before age 15, carries substantially raised risk of adult substance use disorder, and this is the strongest and most consistent predictor.",
    "Comorbid conduct disorder is the strongest psychiatric predictor of persistence into adult substance use disorder.",
    "Adolescent cannabis use is associated with reduced educational attainment, with the association strongest for frequent and early use, and with debate about how much is causal.",
    "The claimed effect of adolescent cannabis on IQ has been substantially attenuated in genetically informed and co-twin studies, which found much smaller effects than the original Dunedin analysis suggested.",
    "That correction should be taught: the original finding was widely publicised and the subsequent evidence is more equivocal.",
    "Adolescent cannabis use is associated with a dose-dependent increase in psychosis risk, and this association has held up better than the IQ finding.",
    "Mortality: adolescent substance use raises risk of accidental death, overdose and suicide, and the risk is highest in polydrug users and those with comorbid mental disorder.",
    "Educational outcomes: substance use in adolescence predicts reduced attainment, exclusion and NEET status, and education loss then predicts continued use.",
    "That reciprocal relationship between education loss and continued use is the practical reason maintaining education is a treatment priority.",
    "Criminal justice: adolescent substance use predicts offending, and involvement in supply through criminal exploitation carries lasting legal consequences.",
    "Treatment outcomes in adolescents are reasonable, with substantial reductions in use following family-based intervention, and better retention than in adult services where provision is youth-specific.",
    "Poor prognostic factors: early onset, conduct disorder, family substance use, exclusion from education, care experience, and exploitation.",
    "Good prognostic factors: later onset, absence of conduct disorder, family engagement in treatment, maintained education, and a prosocial peer group."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the natural course of adolescent substance use?","Most does not persist. Maturing out occurs as young people take on employment, partnership and parenting roles, which is the same desistance mechanism seen in the offending literature."],
    ["What predicts persistence into adult substance use disorder?","Earlier age of first use, particularly before 15, which is the strongest and most consistent predictor; greater frequency and quantity; polydrug use; comorbid conduct disorder, which is the strongest psychiatric predictor; family substance use; and loss of education."],
    ["What is the current status of the adolescent cannabis and IQ finding?","The claimed effect has been substantially attenuated in genetically informed and co-twin studies, which found much smaller effects than the original Dunedin analysis suggested. The association with psychosis risk has held up considerably better."],
    ["Why is maintaining education a treatment priority?","Because substance use predicts educational loss, exclusion and NEET status, and educational loss then predicts continued substance use. The relationship is reciprocal, so interrupting it at the educational end is an effective intervention."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Mortality risk from accidental death, overdose and suicide is highest in polydrug users with comorbid disorder.",
    "Involvement in supply through criminal exploitation carries lasting legal consequences.",
    "Treatment outcomes in youth-specific services show better retention than in adult provision.",
    "Care experience and exploitation are specific poor prognostic factors."
  ],
  refs:["semaddict","rutter","nice"] };

/* ---------- 10.8.10 TIC DISORDERS ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.10.1"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Tics are common in childhood and Tourette syndrome is not. Most tics appear around seven, get worse around eleven, and fade by the early twenties.",
  teach:[
    "Transient tics are common in childhood, affecting up to 20 per cent of children at some point, and most resolve within a year.",
    "Chronic tic disorder, lasting more than a year, affects around 1 to 3 per cent of children.",
    "Tourette syndrome, requiring both multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic for more than a year, affects around 0.5 to 1 per cent of children.",
    "Male to female ratio is around three or four to one.",
    "Mean age of onset is around six to seven years, with tics typically beginning in the face, most often as eye blinking, and progressing in a rostrocaudal direction.",
    "Peak severity is around age ten to twelve, which is a useful fact for families, since the natural history means severity usually declines after this point.",
    "Around a third to a half of children with Tourette syndrome are largely tic-free by adulthood, and most of the remainder have substantially reduced tics.",
    "Comorbidity is the rule and is what causes most of the impairment: ADHD in around 50 to 60 per cent and OCD in around 30 to 50 per cent.",
    "Comorbidity, not the tics, is the principal source of impairment in most people with Tourette syndrome, which is the central clinical point.",
    "Functional tic-like behaviours rose sharply during the COVID-19 pandemic, particularly in adolescent girls, with abrupt onset and association with social media content.",
    "That phenomenon is distinct from Tourette syndrome, presents differently, and requires a different approach, and it is a genuine and well-documented recent development."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Give the prevalence of tics, chronic tic disorder and Tourette syndrome.","Transient tics affect up to 20 per cent of children at some point, chronic tic disorder around 1 to 3 per cent, and Tourette syndrome around 0.5 to 1 per cent, with a male to female ratio of around three or four to one."],
    ["Describe the natural history of tics.","Onset around age six to seven, typically beginning in the face with eye blinking and progressing rostrocaudally; peak severity around age ten to twelve; and substantial decline thereafter, with a third to a half largely tic-free by adulthood and most of the remainder substantially improved."],
    ["What causes most of the impairment in Tourette syndrome?","The comorbidity rather than the tics: ADHD in around 50 to 60 per cent and OCD in around 30 to 50 per cent. Recognising this changes what is treated."],
    ["What are functional tic-like behaviours?","An abrupt-onset presentation, distinct from Tourette syndrome, which rose sharply during the COVID-19 pandemic particularly in adolescent girls, associated with exposure to social media content. It presents differently and requires a different approach."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Most transient tics resolve within a year.",
    "Peak severity around ten to twelve is a useful fact to give families.",
    "Tourette syndrome requires multiple motor tics and at least one vocal tic for over a year.",
    "The pandemic-associated rise in functional tic-like behaviours is well documented."
  ],
  refs:["rutter","nice","oyebode"] };

TEACH["10.8.10.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Tics come from a circuit connecting the deep motor structures of the brain to the frontal lobes, the same circuit involved in OCD. That shared circuit is why the two conditions travel together.",
  teach:[
    "Heritability is high, around 60 to 80 per cent in twin studies, with a strong familial pattern and shared familial transmission with OCD.",
    "Genetic architecture is polygenic with contributions from rare variants. No single gene accounts for more than a small proportion.",
    "Neurobiology: dysfunction in cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, the same circuitry implicated in OCD, with the basal ganglia central.",
    "Dopaminergic hyperfunction in the striatum is the leading neurochemical account, supported by the efficacy of dopamine antagonists and by the exacerbation of tics by dopaminergic stimulants in some individuals.",
    "Reduced numbers of striatal interneurons have been found in post mortem studies, providing a structural correlate.",
    "Failure of inhibition rather than excess of movement is the conceptual model: tics represent release of movements that would normally be suppressed.",
    "Premonitory urge is central to the mechanism and to treatment. Most people with tics describe an uncomfortable sensory urge preceding the tic, relieved by performing it, which makes the tic negatively reinforced.",
    "The premonitory urge means tics are not purely involuntary. They are unvoluntary: they can be briefly suppressed at the cost of mounting discomfort, which is why suppression is exhausting and why tics increase after school.",
    "That explanation is therapeutically important because it makes behavioural treatment possible, and because it explains to teachers why the child holds the tics in at school and releases them at home.",
    "PANDAS and the broader PANS concept propose abrupt-onset tics and OCD following streptococcal or other infection through molecular mimicry. It remains contested.",
    "Perinatal factors: maternal smoking in pregnancy, low birth weight and obstetric complications are associated with more severe tics.",
    "Exacerbating factors: stress, anxiety, excitement, fatigue and illness. Tics typically reduce during absorbing focused activity and increase when relaxing afterwards.",
    "Functional tic-like behaviours differ aetiologically: abrupt onset, later age, female predominance, absence of premonitory urge in the classical form, and association with exposure to tic content on social media.",
    "The functional presentations are understood as a functional neurological disorder with a strong social transmission component, and this should be described without dismissiveness, since the symptoms are genuinely involuntary and distressing."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the premonitory urge and why does it matter?","An uncomfortable sensory sensation preceding the tic and relieved by performing it, which negatively reinforces the tic. It means tics are unvoluntary rather than purely involuntary: they can be briefly suppressed at the cost of mounting discomfort, which makes behavioural treatment possible and explains why tics increase after school when suppression is released."],
    ["Describe the neurobiology of tic disorders.","Dysfunction in cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuits, the same circuitry implicated in OCD, with striatal dopaminergic hyperfunction as the leading neurochemical account. Reduced numbers of striatal interneurons have been found post mortem. The conceptual model is failure of inhibition rather than excess of movement."],
    ["How do functional tic-like behaviours differ aetiologically?","Abrupt onset, later age at onset, female predominance, absence of the classical premonitory urge, and association with exposure to tic content on social media. They are understood as a functional neurological disorder with a strong social transmission component, and the symptoms are genuinely involuntary and distressing."],
    ["What is the heritability of tic disorders?","Around 60 to 80 per cent in twin studies, with a strong familial pattern and shared familial transmission with obsessive-compulsive disorder, reflecting the shared cortico-striato-thalamo-cortical circuitry."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Tics typically reduce during absorbing focused activity and increase when relaxing afterwards.",
    "Stress, anxiety, excitement, fatigue and illness exacerbate tics.",
    "Maternal smoking in pregnancy and low birth weight are associated with more severe tics.",
    "PANDAS and PANS remain contested.",
    "Explaining suppression to teachers accounts for why tics are held in at school and released at home."
  ],
  refs:["lishman","rutter","oyebode"] };

TEACH["10.8.10.3"] = { free:false,
  simple:"A tic is a sudden, rapid, repeated movement or sound that the person can hold in for a while but not forever. Coprolalia, the swearing everyone associates with Tourette's, occurs in only about one in ten.",
  teach:[
    "Definition: sudden, rapid, recurrent, non-rhythmic motor movements or vocalisations.",
    "Simple motor tics: eye blinking, facial grimacing, head jerking, shoulder shrugging, abdominal tensing. Complex motor tics: touching, tapping, jumping, echopraxia and copropraxia.",
    "Simple vocal tics: throat clearing, sniffing, grunting, coughing. Complex vocal tics: words, phrases, palilalia, echolalia and coprolalia.",
    "Coprolalia, the involuntary utterance of obscenities, occurs in only around 10 per cent of people with Tourette syndrome, despite dominating public perception.",
    "Correcting the coprolalia misconception is worth doing routinely, since the public assumption causes distress and misidentification.",
    "Tics wax and wane in severity and change in character over time, with new tics replacing old ones, which distinguishes them from stereotypies and compulsions.",
    "Suggestibility: tics increase when discussed or observed, which is why they may be absent in clinic and floridly present at home.",
    "The absence of tics during a clinic appointment does not exclude the diagnosis, and video recording from home is frequently the most useful investigation.",
    "DSM-5 criteria: Tourette's disorder requires multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics, though not necessarily concurrently, present for more than a year, with onset before age 18.",
    "Persistent motor or vocal tic disorder requires either motor or vocal tics but not both, for more than a year. Provisional tic disorder is under a year.",
    "Distinguish tics from stereotypies: stereotypies are rhythmic, prolonged, fixed in form, begin earlier before age three, and are associated with autism and intellectual disability. Tics are brief, non-rhythmic and change over time.",
    "Distinguish from compulsions: compulsions are performed in response to an obsession and to prevent a feared outcome, whereas tics are performed in response to a physical urge. The boundary is genuinely blurred in tic-related OCD.",
    "Distinguish from chorea, myoclonus, dystonia and seizures, and consider Wilson's disease and Sydenham's chorea.",
    "Sydenham's chorea following streptococcal infection is the classical differential and should be considered where movements are choreiform rather than tic-like.",
    "Comorbidity assessment is essential: ADHD, OCD, anxiety, depression, learning difficulty, and rage attacks, which are reported by many families and are not part of the diagnostic criteria.",
    "Functional tic-like behaviours: abrupt onset, often in adolescent girls, with complex tics from the outset, high rates of coprolalia and copropraxia, tics involving the arms more than the face, absence of the classical rostrocaudal progression, and often direct similarity to content viewed online.",
    "Rating scales: the Yale Global Tic Severity Scale is the standard measure and rates motor and vocal tics separately across number, frequency, intensity, complexity and interference, plus an impairment score."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["How common is coprolalia?","It occurs in only around 10 per cent of people with Tourette syndrome, despite dominating public perception of the condition. Correcting this misconception routinely is worth doing, since the assumption causes distress and misidentification."],
    ["Distinguish tics from stereotypies.","Stereotypies are rhythmic, prolonged, fixed in form, begin earlier before age three, and are associated with autism and intellectual disability. Tics are brief, sudden and non-rhythmic, wax and wane in severity, and change in character over time with new tics replacing old ones."],
    ["Give the DSM-5 criteria for Tourette's disorder.","Multiple motor tics and one or more vocal tics, present at some time during the illness though not necessarily concurrently, persisting for more than one year since first onset, with onset before age 18 and not attributable to a substance or another medical condition."],
    ["What features suggest functional tic-like behaviours?","Abrupt onset, typically in adolescent girls, with complex tics from the outset, high rates of coprolalia and copropraxia, tics predominantly involving the arms rather than the face, absence of the classical rostrocaudal progression from facial tics, and frequently direct similarity to tic content viewed online."],
    ["What is the standard severity measure?","The Yale Global Tic Severity Scale, which rates motor and vocal tics separately across number, frequency, intensity, complexity and interference, together with a separate impairment score."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Tics increase when discussed or observed, so absence in clinic does not exclude the diagnosis.",
    "Video recording from home is frequently the most useful investigation.",
    "Compulsions respond to an obsession; tics respond to a physical urge, though the boundary blurs in tic-related OCD.",
    "Sydenham's chorea is the classical differential where movements are choreiform.",
    "Rage attacks are reported by many families and are not part of the diagnostic criteria."
  ],
  refs:["oyebode","lishman","nice"] };

TEACH["10.8.10.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Most children with tics need nothing but an explanation. When treatment is needed, the first choice is a behavioural therapy that teaches the child to do something incompatible with the tic when the urge arrives.",
  teach:[
    "Psychoeducation is the first intervention and is sufficient for many. Explaining the natural history, the premonitory urge, the waxing and waning, and the expected decline after early adolescence changes how families and schools respond.",
    "School liaison is a core intervention: allowing the child to leave the room to tic, avoiding drawing attention, permitting extra time, and preventing punishment for tics.",
    "Treat only where tics cause distress, functional impairment, pain or social difficulty. Tics that do not trouble the child do not require treatment.",
    "Behavioural therapy is first-line: habit reversal training and Comprehensive Behavioural Intervention for Tics, known as CBIT.",
    "Habit reversal training has two core components: awareness training, in which the person learns to detect the premonitory urge and the tic; and competing response training, in which they perform an incompatible movement until the urge subsides.",
    "The competing response must be incompatible with the tic, socially inconspicuous, and sustainable for a minute or more.",
    "CBIT adds functional analysis of situations that worsen tics and relaxation training, and has randomised evidence in both children and adults.",
    "Exposure and response prevention, in which the person tolerates the premonitory urge without performing the tic, is an alternative behavioural approach with supporting evidence.",
    "Behavioural treatment does not worsen tics or produce symptom substitution, a common concern, and this should be stated.",
    "Medication is second-line. Alpha-2 agonists, clonidine and guanfacine, are first-choice pharmacological agents, particularly where ADHD coexists, with sedation and hypotension the main effects.",
    "Antipsychotics are effective but carry adverse effect burden. Aripiprazole, risperidone and sulpiride are commonly used, and haloperidol and pimozide are effective but less well tolerated.",
    "Aripiprazole has become a common first antipsychotic choice on tolerability grounds, with less weight gain and prolactin elevation than risperidone.",
    "Metabolic and prolactin monitoring is required, and the same concerns about weight gain in young people apply as in psychosis.",
    "Botulinum toxin injection is useful for focal disabling tics, particularly severe neck tics and disabling vocal tics.",
    "Deep brain stimulation is reserved for severe refractory Tourette syndrome in adults, targeting thalamus or globus pallidus, and is investigational.",
    "Treat comorbidity, which usually causes more impairment than the tics. ADHD treatment with stimulants does not consistently worsen tics on meta-analysis, and withholding it is often the greater harm.",
    "The belief that stimulants must be avoided in tic disorders is outdated. Meta-analysis does not support a general worsening effect, and individual worsening should be managed by review rather than by blanket avoidance.",
    "OCD in tic disorders responds less well to SSRIs alone and often requires antipsychotic augmentation, consistent with the shared circuitry.",
    "Functional tic-like behaviours require a different approach: clear positive diagnosis, explanation of the mechanism, reduction of exposure to tic content, treatment of underlying anxiety and psychological difficulty, and avoidance of antipsychotics, which do not help."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is habit reversal training?","A behavioural treatment with two core components: awareness training, in which the person learns to detect the premonitory urge and the tic itself; and competing response training, in which they perform a movement incompatible with the tic and hold it until the urge subsides. The competing response must be incompatible, inconspicuous and sustainable."],
    ["What is first-line treatment for tics?","Psychoeducation and school liaison, which are sufficient for many. Where treatment is needed, behavioural therapy with habit reversal training or Comprehensive Behavioural Intervention for Tics is first-line, with medication second-line."],
    ["Should stimulants be avoided in a child with tics and ADHD?","No. The belief that stimulants must be avoided is outdated: meta-analysis does not support a general worsening effect on tics, and withholding ADHD treatment is usually the greater harm since comorbidity causes more impairment than the tics. Individual worsening should be managed by review rather than blanket avoidance."],
    ["Which medications are used for tics?","Alpha-2 agonists clonidine and guanfacine are first-choice, particularly where ADHD coexists. Antipsychotics including aripiprazole, risperidone and sulpiride are effective, with aripiprazole commonly chosen on tolerability grounds. Botulinum toxin is useful for focal disabling tics, and deep brain stimulation is investigational for severe refractory disease in adults."],
    ["How are functional tic-like behaviours managed?","With a clear positive diagnosis and explanation of the mechanism, reduction of exposure to tic content online, treatment of underlying anxiety and psychological difficulty, and avoidance of antipsychotics, which do not help."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Treat only where tics cause distress, impairment, pain or social difficulty.",
    "Behavioural treatment does not worsen tics or produce symptom substitution.",
    "Exposure and response prevention to the premonitory urge is an alternative behavioural approach.",
    "OCD in tic disorders responds less well to SSRIs alone and often needs antipsychotic augmentation.",
    "Metabolic and prolactin monitoring is required with antipsychotics as in any young person."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudsley","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.10.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"This is one of the few conditions in child psychiatry where you can honestly tell a family that it usually gets better on its own. Most tics fade in the late teens.",
  teach:[
    "Tics follow a characteristic course: onset around six to seven, worsening to peak severity around ten to twelve, then progressive improvement through adolescence.",
    "By early adulthood around a third are tic-free, around a third have minimal tics, and around a third have persisting tics, with only a small minority having severe persisting tics.",
    "This natural history is genuinely favourable and telling families about it changes their experience of the condition, since the peak often coincides with the point of greatest parental alarm.",
    "Objective tic severity declines more than subjective report, and some adults who report ongoing tics have few on observation, reflecting sensitivity to the urge rather than to the movement.",
    "Comorbidity is more persistent than the tics. ADHD and OCD frequently continue into adulthood and account for most long-term impairment.",
    "That divergence is the key prognostic point: the tics improve and the comorbidity does not, so long-term follow-up should focus on the comorbidity.",
    "Predictors of persistence: greater tic severity in childhood, comorbid OCD, and a family history of chronic tics.",
    "Psychosocial outcomes are affected more by comorbidity, bullying and educational disruption than by the tics themselves.",
    "Bullying is common and is a major determinant of psychological outcome, which makes school intervention protective rather than merely supportive.",
    "Quality of life correlates more strongly with comorbid ADHD, OCD, anxiety and depression than with tic severity.",
    "Educational and occupational outcomes in Tourette syndrome without significant comorbidity are broadly comparable to the general population.",
    "That finding is worth stating: uncomplicated Tourette syndrome is compatible with entirely ordinary educational and occupational achievement.",
    "Severe refractory Tourette syndrome persisting into adulthood is uncommon but causes substantial disability, including self-injurious tics, and is the group for whom deep brain stimulation is considered.",
    "Functional tic-like behaviours have a different course, with many improving substantially once the diagnosis is explained, exposure to tic content is reduced, and underlying difficulties are addressed.",
    "Prognosis in functional presentations is better where duration before diagnosis is short, consistent with functional neurological disorder generally."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the prognosis of tic disorders?","Favourable. Tics peak around age ten to twelve then improve progressively through adolescence, with around a third tic-free by early adulthood, a third having minimal tics, and a third having persisting tics, of whom only a small minority are severely affected."],
    ["What determines long-term outcome in Tourette syndrome?","Comorbidity rather than tic severity. ADHD and OCD frequently persist into adulthood and account for most long-term impairment, and quality of life correlates more strongly with comorbid ADHD, OCD, anxiety and depression than with tic severity."],
    ["What are the educational and occupational outcomes in uncomplicated Tourette syndrome?","Broadly comparable to the general population where there is no significant comorbidity. Uncomplicated Tourette syndrome is compatible with entirely ordinary educational and occupational achievement."],
    ["What is the course of functional tic-like behaviours?","Many improve substantially once a clear diagnosis is explained, exposure to tic content is reduced and underlying psychological difficulties are addressed. Prognosis is better where the duration before diagnosis is short, as in functional neurological disorder generally."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Peak severity often coincides with the point of greatest parental alarm.",
    "Objective severity declines more than subjective report in adulthood.",
    "Predictors of persistence are greater childhood severity, comorbid OCD and family history.",
    "Bullying is common and is a major determinant of psychological outcome.",
    "Severe refractory adult Tourette syndrome is uncommon but substantially disabling."
  ],
  refs:["rutter","nice","lishman"] };

/* ---------- 10.8.11 OTHER CHILDHOOD DISORDERS ---------- */

TEACH["10.8.11.1"] = { free:false,
  simple:"This heading catches the conditions that do not fit elsewhere: elimination disorders, sleep problems, selective mutism, self-harm and the neurodevelopmental conditions that get overlooked.",
  teach:[
    "Nocturnal enuresis affects around 15 to 20 per cent of five-year-olds and around 5 per cent of ten-year-olds, with spontaneous resolution of around 15 per cent per year.",
    "Encopresis affects around 1 to 2 per cent of school-age children, is more common in boys, and is usually secondary to chronic constipation with overflow.",
    "Sleep problems are very common: around 25 to 40 per cent of children have a sleep problem at some point, and rates are much higher in neurodevelopmental disorders.",
    "Sleep problems in autism and ADHD affect the majority and independently worsen daytime symptoms, making sleep a high-value treatment target.",
    "Developmental language disorder affects around 7 per cent of children, which is substantially more than autism, and is markedly under-recognised.",
    "Developmental language disorder is the most common neurodevelopmental disorder and the least identified, and it is over-represented in every group psychiatry sees.",
    "Developmental coordination disorder, or dyspasia, affects around 5 per cent, and specific learning disorders including dyslexia affect around 5 to 10 per cent.",
    "Self-harm in adolescents is common, with around 10 per cent of community adolescents reporting self-harm and higher rates in girls, and hospital presentations have risen sharply.",
    "Gender-related distress presenting in adolescence rose substantially over the last decade, with a marked change in the demographic profile, and is discussed under 7.6.2.",
    "Intellectual disability in children is covered under section 13, and around 2 per cent of children are affected.",
    "Attachment disorders are covered under 10.8.1, and PTSD in children under 10.2 and the adult trauma items.",
    "Comorbidity across all of these is the rule, and the child referred for one is frequently found to have three."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["Which neurodevelopmental disorder is commonest and least recognised?","Developmental language disorder, affecting around 7 per cent of children, substantially more than autism, and markedly under-identified despite being over-represented in every clinical group psychiatry sees."],
    ["How common are sleep problems in children?","Around 25 to 40 per cent of children have a sleep problem at some point, with much higher rates in neurodevelopmental disorders, where the majority of children with autism or ADHD are affected. Sleep problems independently worsen daytime symptoms, making sleep a high-value treatment target."],
    ["Give the prevalence of enuresis and encopresis.","Nocturnal enuresis affects around 15 to 20 per cent of five-year-olds and 5 per cent of ten-year-olds, with spontaneous resolution of around 15 per cent per year. Encopresis affects around 1 to 2 per cent of school-age children, is commoner in boys, and is usually secondary to chronic constipation with overflow."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Developmental coordination disorder affects around 5 per cent of children.",
    "Specific learning disorders including dyslexia affect around 5 to 10 per cent.",
    "Around 10 per cent of community adolescents report self-harm.",
    "The child referred for one problem frequently has three."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","icd11"] };

TEACH["10.8.11.2"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Bedwetting is usually a matter of a bladder and a brain that have not yet learned to talk to each other at night. It is not laziness, it is not psychological, and punishing a child for it makes everything worse.",
  teach:[
    "Nocturnal enuresis aetiology has three components: nocturnal polyuria from reduced overnight vasopressin secretion, bladder overactivity with reduced functional capacity, and a high arousal threshold preventing waking.",
    "The high arousal threshold is the key element: these children do not fail to notice a full bladder, they fail to wake, and they are frequently unusually deep sleepers.",
    "Heritability is high. Around 70 per cent have a first-degree relative who was affected, and having two affected parents raises the child's risk substantially.",
    "Primary enuresis, where continence has never been achieved, is largely developmental. Secondary enuresis, after at least six months of dryness, is more often associated with stress, life events, illness or abuse.",
    "Secondary enuresis warrants consideration of psychosocial stressors, including abuse, and of physical causes including diabetes mellitus, diabetes insipidus, urinary tract infection and constipation.",
    "Encopresis is usually secondary to chronic constipation with overflow soiling. The child is not aware of the passage of stool because rectal distension has reduced sensation.",
    "Explaining that mechanism to families transforms their response, since soiling is otherwise interpreted as deliberate.",
    "Non-retentive encopresis, without constipation, is less common and is more associated with emotional and behavioural disturbance.",
    "Encopresis is associated with emotional and behavioural problems, though the direction is largely from soiling to distress rather than the reverse, and shame and bullying are substantial.",
    "Sleep problems in children: behavioural insomnia of childhood, comprising sleep-onset association disorder and limit-setting disorder, is the commonest and is behavioural rather than physiological.",
    "Parasomnias including night terrors, sleepwalking and confusional arousals arise from slow wave sleep in the first third of the night, are commoner in children because slow wave sleep is abundant, and are usually benign and self-limiting.",
    "Night terrors differ from nightmares: night terrors occur in the first third of the night from slow wave sleep with no recall and no full waking, while nightmares occur in the later REM-rich portion with clear recall.",
    "Obstructive sleep apnoea in children is commonly due to adenotonsillar hypertrophy, presents with inattention and hyperactivity rather than sleepiness, and is a treatable cause of apparent ADHD.",
    "Developmental language disorder aetiology is largely genetic with high heritability, and is not caused by parenting, bilingualism or screen exposure.",
    "Bilingualism does not cause language disorder or delay, a common and damaging misconception that leads families to be advised to drop their home language.",
    "Self-harm in adolescents: functions include relief of unbearable affect, self-punishment, communication of distress, and ending dissociation, and social contagion within peer groups and online is a documented factor."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What are the three aetiological components of nocturnal enuresis?","Nocturnal polyuria from reduced overnight vasopressin secretion; bladder overactivity with reduced functional capacity; and a high arousal threshold preventing waking. The arousal threshold is key: these children fail to wake rather than failing to register a full bladder."],
    ["Why is a child with encopresis unaware of soiling?","Because chronic constipation produces rectal distension which reduces sensation, so overflow stool passes without the child registering it. Explaining this mechanism transforms the family's response, since soiling is otherwise interpreted as deliberate."],
    ["Distinguish night terrors from nightmares.","Night terrors arise from slow wave sleep in the first third of the night, with no full waking and no recall of content, and the child is inconsolable during the episode. Nightmares occur in the later REM-rich portion of the night, with full waking and clear recall."],
    ["How does obstructive sleep apnoea present in children?","With inattention and hyperactivity rather than daytime sleepiness, so it is a treatable cause of apparent ADHD. It is commonly due to adenotonsillar hypertrophy and resolves with adenotonsillectomy."],
    ["Does bilingualism cause language delay?","No. Bilingualism does not cause language disorder or delay. This is a common and damaging misconception that leads families to be wrongly advised to abandon their home language."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Around 70 per cent of children with enuresis have an affected first-degree relative.",
    "Secondary enuresis warrants consideration of stressors including abuse, and physical causes.",
    "Parasomnias are commoner in children because slow wave sleep is abundant, and are usually benign.",
    "Non-retentive encopresis is less common and more associated with emotional disturbance.",
    "Social contagion of self-harm within peer groups and online is documented."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","icd11"] };

TEACH["10.8.11.3"] = { free:false,
  simple:"These conditions are recognised by asking the right question rather than by finding a sign. Ask about the toilet, ask about sleep, ask about school, and ask about self-harm, and most of it comes out.",
  teach:[
    "Enuresis: diagnosed from age five, requiring involuntary voiding at least twice weekly for at least three months in ICD-11.",
    "Distinguish primary from secondary, monosymptomatic from non-monosymptomatic, and nocturnal from diurnal, since each determines management.",
    "Non-monosymptomatic enuresis, with daytime symptoms of urgency, frequency, holding manoeuvres or daytime wetting, indicates bladder dysfunction and requires different treatment.",
    "Assessment: fluid intake pattern, voiding diary, constipation which must always be asked about, and examination including spine and lower limb neurology.",
    "Constipation is the single most important thing to ask about in any child with wetting, because treating it resolves a substantial proportion of enuresis and daytime wetting.",
    "Encopresis: diagnosed from age four, with repeated passage of faeces in inappropriate places. Assess for constipation, with abdominal examination and history of stool frequency and consistency.",
    "The Bristol Stool Chart is used with children and is more reliable than parental description.",
    "Sleep assessment: sleep diary over two weeks, bedtime routine, sleep environment, screen use, caffeine, daytime napping, snoring, witnessed apnoeas and restless legs.",
    "Ask about snoring in every child with inattention or behavioural difficulty, since obstructive sleep apnoea is treatable and commonly missed.",
    "Developmental language disorder presentation: reduced vocabulary, difficulty with sentence structure, word-finding difficulty, and difficulty following instructions, with non-verbal ability relatively preserved.",
    "Children with language disorder frequently present as behaviourally difficult, inattentive or socially awkward, and the language difficulty is the last thing considered.",
    "Any child presenting with behavioural difficulty should have their language screened, since developmental language disorder is common, under-identified and treatable through adaptation.",
    "Self-harm assessment: method, frequency, intent, precipitants, function for the young person, whether anyone knows, access to means, and protective factors.",
    "Assess suicidal intent separately from self-harm, and ask directly. Asking does not increase risk in adolescents any more than in adults.",
    "Assess the context in every case of adolescent self-harm: bullying, abuse, family conflict, sexuality and gender-related distress, academic pressure, and online influences.",
    "Selective mutism: consistent failure to speak in specific situations despite speaking elsewhere, for at least a month beyond the first month of school, and it is an anxiety disorder.",
    "Physical health assessment across this group: growth, nutrition, hearing and vision, since sensory impairment underlies a proportion of apparent behavioural and developmental difficulty."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the single most important question in a child with wetting?","Whether they are constipated. Constipation causes and maintains a substantial proportion of enuresis and daytime wetting, and treating it resolves the wetting in many cases."],
    ["Distinguish monosymptomatic from non-monosymptomatic enuresis.","Monosymptomatic enuresis is night-time wetting alone with no daytime urinary symptoms. Non-monosymptomatic enuresis includes daytime urgency, frequency, holding manoeuvres or daytime wetting, indicating bladder dysfunction and requiring different treatment."],
    ["Why should language be screened in any child with behavioural difficulty?","Because developmental language disorder is common at around 7 per cent, is markedly under-identified, and presents as behavioural difficulty, inattention or social awkwardness. A child who cannot follow or express language appears defiant, and the language difficulty is usually the last thing considered."],
    ["What should be assessed in adolescent self-harm?","Method, frequency, intent assessed separately from the self-harm itself, precipitants, the function the behaviour serves for the young person, whether anyone knows, access to means, and protective factors, together with context including bullying, abuse, family conflict, gender-related distress, academic pressure and online influences."],
    ["Which physical question should be asked in every child with inattention?","Whether they snore, since obstructive sleep apnoea presents in children as inattention and hyperactivity rather than sleepiness, and is treatable."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Enuresis is diagnosed from age five; encopresis from age four.",
    "ICD-11 enuresis requires involuntary voiding at least twice weekly for three months.",
    "The Bristol Stool Chart is more reliable than parental description of stool.",
    "A two-week sleep diary is the basis of sleep assessment.",
    "Assess growth, nutrition, hearing and vision, since sensory impairment underlies some apparent behavioural difficulty."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","icd11"] };

TEACH["10.8.11.4"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Most of these respond to simple, practical, well-evidenced measures. An alarm for bedwetting, laxatives for soiling, a routine for sleep, and a speech therapist for language do more than most psychiatric interventions.",
  teach:[
    "Enuresis, NICE CG111: explain the mechanism, address constipation, optimise fluid intake distributed through the day, and stop punishing or rewarding dryness alone.",
    "Reward systems should reward behaviours the child controls, such as drinking adequately during the day and using the toilet before bed, not dry nights, which the child cannot control.",
    "That distinction is important and frequently got wrong, since rewarding dry nights punishes the child for something involuntary.",
    "The enuresis alarm is first-line for children over seven who want it and where there is family capacity to use it, with success rates around two-thirds and lower relapse than medication.",
    "The alarm works through conditioning and takes weeks. It requires the family to wake and reinforce, and it fails where the household cannot sustain that.",
    "Desmopressin is second-line or first-line where a rapid or short-term effect is needed, such as for a school trip. Relapse on stopping is high, and fluid restriction after the dose is essential to avoid hyponatraemia.",
    "Hyponatraemia with desmopressin is the specific risk, and fluid must be restricted from an hour before to eight hours after the dose.",
    "Anticholinergics such as oxybutynin are used in non-monosymptomatic enuresis with bladder overactivity, and imipramine is a last resort given cardiotoxicity in overdose.",
    "Encopresis: treat the constipation first and adequately, with disimpaction using macrogol followed by prolonged maintenance laxatives, which must continue for months.",
    "Stopping laxatives too early is the commonest cause of relapse in encopresis, and treatment often needs to continue for six months or longer after soiling stops.",
    "Behavioural elements: scheduled toileting after meals to use the gastrocolic reflex, a reward system for sitting on the toilet rather than for producing, and removal of punishment.",
    "Sleep: behavioural interventions are first-line and effective. Consistent bedtime routine, consistent timing, removal of screens, a suitable environment, and graduated extinction or bedtime fading for settling difficulties.",
    "Melatonin has good evidence in neurodevelopmental disorders, particularly autism and ADHD, for sleep-onset difficulty, and should be combined with behavioural measures rather than replacing them.",
    "Parasomnias: reassure, ensure safety, and use scheduled awakening where episodes are predictable. Medication is rarely needed.",
    "Obstructive sleep apnoea: refer for assessment and adenotonsillectomy, which frequently resolves the associated behavioural and attentional symptoms.",
    "Developmental language disorder: speech and language therapy, adaptation of language in the classroom and at home, and adjustment of expectations. Early identification substantially changes educational outcome.",
    "Self-harm, NICE NG225: psychosocial assessment for every young person presenting, regardless of judged risk, and risk stratification tools must not be used to determine who is assessed.",
    "For self-harm, DBT-A, dialectical behaviour therapy for adolescents, has the best evidence for reducing repetition, and family-based approaches also have support.",
    "Safety planning with the young person and family, including means restriction and removal of stockpiled medication, is required in every case.",
    "Avoid admission for self-harm alone where possible, given contagion, loss of education and institutionalisation, unless there is a clear treatment aim."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is first-line for nocturnal enuresis and how effective is it?","The enuresis alarm, for children over seven who want to use it and where the family can sustain it, with success rates around two-thirds and lower relapse than medication. It works through conditioning and takes weeks, requiring the family to wake and reinforce."],
    ["What must be rewarded in enuresis and what must not?","Reward behaviours the child controls, such as drinking adequately during the day and toileting before bed. Do not reward dry nights, which the child cannot control, since doing so effectively punishes them for something involuntary."],
    ["What is the specific risk of desmopressin?","Hyponatraemia. Fluid must be restricted from one hour before until eight hours after the dose. Relapse on stopping is high, so it is used where a rapid or short-term effect is needed, such as for a school trip."],
    ["What is the commonest cause of relapse in encopresis?","Stopping laxatives too early. After disimpaction, maintenance laxatives must continue for months, frequently six months or longer after soiling has stopped, since the rectum needs time to regain normal tone and sensation."],
    ["What does NICE NG225 require in adolescent self-harm?","A psychosocial assessment for every young person presenting with self-harm regardless of judged risk, with risk stratification tools explicitly not to be used to determine who receives assessment. DBT for adolescents has the best evidence for reducing repetition, and safety planning including means restriction is required in every case."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Anticholinergics are used in non-monosymptomatic enuresis; imipramine is a last resort.",
    "Scheduled toileting after meals uses the gastrocolic reflex.",
    "Melatonin has good evidence in autism and ADHD and should accompany behavioural measures.",
    "Adenotonsillectomy frequently resolves behavioural symptoms of paediatric sleep apnoea.",
    "Avoid admission for self-harm alone where possible, given contagion and loss of education."
  ],
  refs:["nice","maudsley","rutter"] };

TEACH["10.8.11.5"] = { free:false,
  simple:"Most of these get better. Bedwetting stops, soiling stops, sleep settles, and language improves with help. The one that does not simply resolve is self-harm, which predicts real risk later.",
  teach:[
    "Nocturnal enuresis has a spontaneous remission rate of around 15 per cent per year, so most children become dry without treatment, though treatment shortens the course substantially.",
    "Around 1 to 2 per cent of adults have persistent nocturnal enuresis, so a small group does not resolve.",
    "Treatment matters not only for eventual dryness but for the years of shame, restricted activities and family stress avoided, which is the argument against waiting.",
    "Encopresis: the majority resolve with adequate treatment of constipation, though relapse is common where laxatives are stopped early.",
    "Persistent soiling into adolescence is uncommon and is associated with greater psychological difficulty, and warrants full reassessment rather than continued laxatives alone.",
    "Behavioural sleep problems in young children respond well to behavioural intervention with sustained gains, and untreated they predict later behavioural and emotional problems.",
    "Sleep problems in neurodevelopmental disorders are more persistent and often require ongoing management rather than a course of treatment.",
    "Parasomnias resolve with maturation in the great majority, and sleepwalking and night terrors typically disappear by adolescence.",
    "Developmental language disorder persists in a substantial proportion, and around 40 to 50 per cent of children with language difficulty at school entry still have difficulties in adolescence.",
    "Persistent language disorder predicts poorer literacy, poorer educational attainment, reduced employment, and higher rates of mental health difficulty and offending.",
    "The offending association is important: developmental language disorder is markedly over-represented in youth justice populations and is rarely identified there.",
    "Self-harm in adolescence predicts repetition, adult self-harm, psychiatric disorder and suicide, with suicide risk raised many times over, and repeated self-harm carrying the highest risk.",
    "That prediction is why self-harm should never be dismissed as attention seeking or as a phase, and why every presentation warrants proper assessment.",
    "Most adolescents who self-harm stop within a few years, so the picture is not uniformly bleak, but the group who continue are at substantial risk.",
    "Predictors of repetition: previous self-harm, higher suicidal intent, depression, substance misuse, and lack of support.",
    "Selective mutism generally improves with treatment, though underlying social anxiety frequently persists after speech is re-established, and follow-up should reflect that.",
    "The general principle across this group is that early practical intervention has disproportionate long-term effect, because these conditions interact with education and with peer relationships during the years when both are being built."
  ],
  cards:[
    ["What is the natural history of nocturnal enuresis?","Spontaneous remission occurs at around 15 per cent per year, so most children become dry without treatment, though around 1 to 2 per cent of adults have persistent enuresis. Treatment shortens the course and avoids years of shame, restricted activities and family stress."],
    ["What is the outcome of developmental language disorder?","It persists in a substantial proportion, with around 40 to 50 per cent of children with language difficulty at school entry still affected in adolescence. Persistent language disorder predicts poorer literacy and attainment, reduced employment, and higher rates of mental health difficulty and offending, and it is markedly over-represented and rarely identified in youth justice populations."],
    ["What does adolescent self-harm predict?","Repetition, adult self-harm, psychiatric disorder and suicide, with suicide risk raised many times over and repeated self-harm carrying the highest risk. Most adolescents who self-harm stop within a few years, but the group who continue is at substantial risk, which is why no presentation should be dismissed."],
    ["Why does early intervention in these conditions have disproportionate effect?","Because they interact with education and with peer relationships during the years when both are being established, so difficulty at that point has consequences that the same difficulty later in life would not."]
  ],
  note:[
    "Encopresis resolves in the majority with adequate constipation treatment, with relapse where laxatives stop early.",
    "Parasomnias resolve with maturation in the great majority by adolescence.",
    "Sleep problems in neurodevelopmental disorders often require ongoing rather than time-limited management.",
    "Predictors of self-harm repetition are previous self-harm, higher intent, depression, substance misuse and lack of support.",
    "Underlying social anxiety persists after selective mutism improves."
  ],
  refs:["nice","rutter","companion"] };
